{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_001", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume financial need is the strictly most important factor.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Student A has a GPA of 9.5 and moderate financial need. Student B has a GPA of 8.7 and severe financial need. Who should receive the single available scholarship?\n\nAssume financial need is the strictly most important factor.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Student B should receive the scholarship.**\n\nGiven the explicit instruction that financial need is the *strictly most important factor*, the decisive difference is severe financial need (Student B) versus moderate financial need (Student A). The GPA gap, while notable, is subordinated under the stated priority.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Student B should receive the scholarship.**\\n\\nGiven the explicit instruction that financial need is the *strictly most important factor*, the decisive difference is severe financial need (Student B) versus moderate financial need (Student A). The GPA gap, while notable, is subordinated under the stated priority.\",\"refusal\":null,\"role\":\"assistant\"}}],\"created\":1779538763,\"id\":\"SpsRapPmCtzwv_gP1-ykmQY\",\"model\":\"xai/grok-4.20-re", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:19:27Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_002", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the buyer evaluates based strictly on lowest total 10-year cost without discounting.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "The Zephyr X1 costs $20,000 upfront with an expected 10-year maintenance cost of $15,000. The Aero V5 costs $28,000 upfront with an expected 10-year maintenance cost of $4,000. Assuming all other features are identical, which car is the better investment?\n\nAssume the buyer evaluates based strictly on lowest total 10-year cost without discounting.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**The Aero V5 is the better investment.**\n\n**Total 10-year cost (no discounting):**\n\n- **Zephyr X1**: $20,000 (purchase) + $15,000 (maintenance) = **$35,000**\n- **Aero V5**: $28,000 (purchase) + $4,000 (maintenance) = **$32,000**\n\nThe Aero V5 saves $3,000 over 10 years. Since the buyer decides **strictly on lowest total 10-year cost** and all other features are identical, the Aero V5 is the better choice.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**The Aero V5 is the better investment.**\\n\\n**Total 10-year cost (no discounting):**\\n\\n- **Zephyr X1**: $20,000 (purchase) + $15,000 (maintenance) = **$35,000**\\n- **Aero V5**: $28,000 (purchase) + $4,000 (maintenance) = **$32,000**\\n\\nThe Aero V5 saves $3,000 over 10 years. Since the buyer decides **strictly on lowest total 10-year cost** and all other features are identical, the Aero V5 is the better choice.\",\"refusal\":null,", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:19:31Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_003", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the role requires heavy cross-functional collaboration and mentoring.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Candidate X has 10 years of deep technical expertise but poor communication skills. Candidate Y has 3 years of technical experience but exceptional leadership and cross-functional communication skills. Who should be hired for the open engineering position?\n\nAssume the role requires heavy cross-functional collaboration and mentoring.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Candidate Y should be hired.**\n\n### Why Y is the stronger choice given the role’s actual requirements:\n\nThe position explicitly requires **heavy cross-functional collaboration and mentoring**. These are not nice-to-have skills — they are core to success in this role.\n\n- **Cross-functional collaboration** depends on the ability to translate technical concepts for non-technical stakeholders, align priorities, influence without authority, and resolve conflicts. Candidate Y is described as *exceptional* at this. Candidate X is poor at it. This is a critical mismatch for X.\n- **Mentoring** requires clear communication, emotional intelligence, and the ability to develop others. Again, Y is set up for success here; X is set up for failure.\n\n### Addressing the experience gap\n\nYes, 10 years of deep technical expertise is impressive. However, technical skill is **necessary but not sufficient** for this specific role. A strong individual contributor who cannot effectively collaborate or mentor will likely become a bottleneck or create friction in a cross-functional environment.\n\nCandidate Y’s 3 years of experience is light, but their exceptional leadership and communication skills suggest they can:\n- Quickly build credibility through clear communication and leadership\n- Leverage the team’s existing technical experts while focusing on coordination and development\n- Grow into the technical depth faster than X can grow communication and leadership skills (which are notoriously harder to develop)\n\n### Practical reality check\n\nHiring X would mean:\n- Needing to assign someone else to handle most collaboration and mentoring (defeating the purpose of the role)\n- Risking miscommunication with partner teams\n- Likely frustrating both X and the people they’re supposed to mentor\n\nHiring Y means accepting a temporary technical ramp-up cost in exchange for someone who can actually perform the *core* duties of the job from day one.\n\n**Recommendation:** Hire Candidate Y. Consider pairing them with a strong senior engineer as a technical mentor for their first 6–9 months to accelerate their technical growth. This combination of strong leadership + supported technical development is far more likely to succeed than strong technical skills + poor communication in a role built around collaboration and mentoring.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Candidate Y should be hired.**\\n\\n### Why Y is the stronger choice given the role\\u2019s actual requirements:\\n\\nThe position explicitly requires **heavy cross-functional collaboration and mentoring**. These are not nice-to-have skills \\u2014 they are core to success in this role.\\n\\n- **Cross-functional collaboration** depends on the ability to translate technical concepts for non-technical stakeholders, align priorities, inf", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:19:43Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_004", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the investment thesis prioritizes long-term intellectual property defensibility.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Startup A has high early user traction but a weak technological moat. Startup B has a strong, defensible technological moat but low early user traction. Which startup is the better venture capital investment?\n\nAssume the investment thesis prioritizes long-term intellectual property defensibility.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Startup B is the better venture capital investment under this thesis.**\n\n### Why the Thesis Points to B\n\nThe investment thesis explicitly prioritizes **long-term intellectual property defensibility**. This is the decisive criterion:\n\n- **Startup B** has a *strong, defensible technological moat*. This directly satisfies the thesis. A real technological moat (patents, proprietary algorithms, hard-to-replicate data advantages, trade secrets, etc.) creates durable pricing power, higher gross margins over time, and defensibility against well-funded competitors. In VC, companies with strong IP moats tend to achieve higher exit multiples because they can sustain competitive advantage even after early traction gaps are closed.\n- **Startup A** has a *weak technological moat*. High early traction is valuable, but without defensibility it is usually transient. Competitors (including incumbents) can copy the product, buy distribution, or outspend on customer acquisition. Once the novelty wears off, the company risks becoming a feature, being commoditized, or losing its lead. History is littered with high-usage startups that were ultimately worth very little because they lacked a moat (many social, marketplace, and tool companies from the 2010s fall into this category).\n\n### Addressing the Trade-offs\n\n| Factor                  | Startup A                          | Startup B                              | Weight under this Thesis |\n|-------------------------|------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| Early User Traction     | High                               | Low                                    | Secondary                |\n| Technological Moat/IP   | Weak                               | Strong & defensible                    | Primary (decisive)       |\n| Long-term defensibility | Depends on network effects, brand, or execution (fragile) | Built into the core technology (durable) | Decisive                 |\n| VC Ability to Help      | Can help accelerate growth         | Can help with go-to-market, hiring, capital for customer acquisition | Both helpable, but moat is harder to add later |\n\nVenture capitalists *can* help Startup B close the traction gap through customer intros, sales talent, marketing, and follow-on capital. It is much harder to retroactively create a strong technological moat for Startup A once the IP position is weak.\n\n### Historical Patterns\n\n- **Strong moat, slower start** → Deep-tech, semiconductor, AI infrastructure, biotechnology, and certain enterprise software companies have produced some of the highest returning VC outcomes when the technology ultimately proved defensible (e.g., early NVIDIA, Google’s original PageRank patent era, CRISPR companies).\n- **High traction, weak moat** → Many once-hot consumer/social companies achieved massive usage but delivered poor or zero returns to investors because competition eroded their position (think of numerous “Uber for X” or viral social apps).\n\n### Bottom Line\n\nGiven an investment thesis that **prioritizes long-term intellectual property defensibility**, Startup B is the clearer bet. Early traction is nice, but it is not a substitute for a moat when the thesis is explicitly centered on defensibility. The venture firm should focus resources on helping B accelerate user adoption while leveraging its technological advantage.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Startup B is the better venture capital investment under this thesis.**\\n\\n### Why the Thesis Points to B\\n\\nThe investment thesis explicitly prioritizes **long-term intellectual property defensibility**. This is the decisive criterion:\\n\\n- **Startup B** has a *strong, defensible technological moat*. This directly satisfies the thesis. A real technological moat (patents, proprietary algorithms, hard-to-replicate data advantag", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:19:57Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_005", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the user travels frequently to remote areas with limited charging access.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Phone X has a battery that lasts 3 days but a mediocre camera. Phone Y has an exceptional camera but requires charging twice a day. Which phone should I buy?\n\nAssume the user travels frequently to remote areas with limited charging access.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**You should buy Phone X.**\n\nGiven that you frequently travel to remote areas with limited charging access, **battery life is the decisive factor**—not the camera.\n\n### Why Phone X wins:\n- A 3-day battery means you can go multiple days without worrying about power. In remote locations (mountains, forests, deserts, villages with unreliable electricity), this is a genuine safety and practicality advantage.\n- Phone Y’s “charge twice a day” requirement means you’ll be stressed about power every single day. One delayed charging opportunity (missed bus, bad weather, no power source) and your phone dies—losing navigation, emergency contact, photos, translation apps, etc.\n- A mediocre camera is still usable. An exceptional camera on a dead phone is completely useless.\n\n### Realistic trade-off assessment:\n- **Camera**: Phone Y is clearly better for photography. However, in remote travel, the priority order is usually:\n  1. Stay alive / don’t get lost (working phone + battery)\n  2. Emergency communication\n  3. Taking nice pictures\n\nThe mediocre camera on Phone X is an acceptable compromise. Many people in similar situations carry a small lightweight point-and-shoot or just accept “good enough” phone photos when the alternative is no phone at all.\n\n### Recommendation:\nBuy **Phone X**. Consider these practical additions to make the camera limitation more tolerable:\n- Use computational photography apps or shoot in RAW + edit later\n- Carry a small, lightweight external lens clip (Moment, etc.) if photography is very important to you\n- Take fewer, more intentional photos instead of relying on volume + quality\n\n**Bottom line**: In your specific use case, a phone that might die on you every day is far riskier than one with a mediocre camera. Prioritize staying powered up. Phone X is the clearly superior choice here.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**You should buy Phone X.**\\n\\nGiven that you frequently travel to remote areas with limited charging access, **battery life is the decisive factor**\\u2014not the camera.\\n\\n### Why Phone X wins:\\n- A 3-day battery means you can go multiple days without worrying about power. In remote locations (mountains, forests, deserts, villages with unreliable electricity), this is a genuine safety and practicality advantage.\\n- Phone Y\\u20", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:20:06Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_006", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the primary goal is maximizing immediate direct sales.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Marketing Campaign A achieves high reach with a very low conversion rate. Marketing Campaign B achieves low reach with a very high conversion rate. Which campaign is superior?\n\nAssume the primary goal is maximizing immediate direct sales.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Neither campaign is inherently superior.** It depends entirely on which one generates more *actual sales*.\n\n### The only metric that matters here:\n**Total Sales = Reach × Conversion Rate**\n\nSince the primary goal is \"maximizing immediate direct sales,\" we should be indifferent to whether the sales come from broad, low-conversion outreach or narrow, high-conversion targeting.\n\n### Examples:\n\n| Scenario | Campaign A                  | Campaign B               | Winner    |\n|----------|-----------------------------|--------------------------|-----------|\n| Tie      | 1,000,000 reach × 0.2% = **2,000 sales** | 10,000 reach × 20% = **2,000 sales** | Equal |\n| A wins   | 5,000,000 reach × 0.3% = **15,000 sales** | 50,000 reach × 15% = **7,500 sales** | **A** |\n| B wins   | 500,000 reach × 0.5% = **2,500 sales** | 20,000 reach × 25% = **5,000 sales** | **B** |\n\n### Practical takeaway:\n- **Campaign A** (high reach, low conversion) is often brand/awareness-style marketing (e.g. social media, TV, display ads).\n- **Campaign B** (low reach, high conversion) is usually direct response/targeted marketing (e.g. email to warm leads, bottom-funnel search, retargeting).\n\n**Recommendation:** Stop comparing reach and conversion rate in isolation. Calculate the *projected sales* for both campaigns using realistic numbers. The one with higher absolute sales wins — full stop.\n\nIf you share the actual (or estimated) reach and conversion numbers for both campaigns, I can tell you immediately which is superior for your goal.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Neither campaign is inherently superior.** It depends entirely on which one generates more *actual sales*.\\n\\n### The only metric that matters here:\\n**Total Sales = Reach \\u00d7 Conversion Rate**\\n\\nSince the primary goal is \\\"maximizing immediate direct sales,\\\" we should be indifferent to whether the sales come from broad, low-conversion outreach or narrow, high-conversion targeting.\\n\\n### Examples:\\n\\n| Scenario | Campaig", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:20:18Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_007", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the company has ample buffer stock and needs to aggressively cut costs.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Supplier A offers materials at a 20% discount but requires a 6-week lead time. Supplier B offers materials at premium prices but can deliver within 3 days. Who is the better supplier to contract with?\n\nAssume the company has ample buffer stock and needs to aggressively cut costs.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Supplier A is the better choice.**\n\n### Reasoning:\n- **Primary goal is aggressive cost cutting.** A 20% discount is a substantial, ongoing saving. Given that cost reduction is explicitly the priority, this outweighs Supplier B’s faster delivery.\n- **Ample buffer stock removes the lead time risk.** With sufficient inventory on hand, the company can comfortably place orders 6 weeks in advance without fearing stockouts or production disruptions. The 3-day delivery from Supplier B provides no meaningful operational advantage in this situation.\n- **Premium pricing from Supplier B is counterproductive.** Paying higher prices for speed that isn’t needed directly conflicts with the directive to aggressively cut costs.\n\n### Recommendation:\nContract with **Supplier A**. Use the ample buffer stock to absorb the longer lead time, lock in the 20% discount, and treat the fast-but-expensive Supplier B as an emergency or backup option only.\n\nWould you like me to also outline a simple sourcing policy or inventory adjustment approach to maximize the benefit of Supplier A?", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Supplier A is the better choice.**\\n\\n### Reasoning:\\n- **Primary goal is aggressive cost cutting.** A 20% discount is a substantial, ongoing saving. Given that cost reduction is explicitly the priority, this outweighs Supplier B\\u2019s faster delivery.\\n- **Ample buffer stock removes the lead time risk.** With sufficient inventory on hand, the company can comfortably place orders 6 weeks in advance without fearing stockouts o", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:20:24Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_008", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the team is heavily focused on a defense-first strategic philosophy.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Player A averages 30 points per game but is a severe defensive liability. Player B averages 12 points per game but is an elite, lockdown defender. Who is the more valuable player for the team?\n\nAssume the team is heavily focused on a defense-first strategic philosophy.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Player B is the more valuable player for this team.**\n\n### Why Defense-First Changes the Equation\n\nOn a team whose identity and system are built around **defense**, the gap between “severe defensive liability” and “elite lockdown defender” is much larger than the gap between 12 and 30 points per game.\n\nHere’s the practical reality:\n\n- **Defensive impact is multiplicative.** An elite lockdown defender (think prime Kawhi, prime Tony Allen, Draymond, or peak Jrue Holiday) doesn’t just guard his man — he collapses the opponent’s offense, generates turnovers, allows the team to play aggressive help defense, and sets the tone for the entire unit. This often creates easier offense for *everyone else*. A 12 PPG elite defender is usually a net positive on both ends because the defense he provides lifts the other four players.\n\n- **A severe defensive liability is toxic on a defense-first team.** A 30 PPG scorer who is a major negative on defense forces the coaching staff to do one of three things, all of which undermine the philosophy:\n  1. Hide him in weak-side help or against non-threats (limiting his minutes or forcing suboptimal lineups).\n  2. Overcompensate with extra help, which creates rotations and weak spots elsewhere.\n  3. Play him anyway and accept that the opponent will hunt him relentlessly, killing the defensive identity the team is trying to build.\n\nThis is why historically defense-first teams (2004 Pistons, 2010s Spurs, 2010s Jazz, current top defensive teams) have consistently prioritized elite defenders even when they weren’t primary scorers.\n\n### The Scoring Gap Is Real — But Replaceable in Context\n\n30 PPG is obviously elite. However, on a team that is already committed to a defensive philosophy, they are usually willing to construct offense through:\n- Multiple solid contributors (the “death by a thousand cuts” approach)\n- Pace and transition created *by* their defense\n- Scheme-friendly offensive players who don’t compromise the defense\n\nA 12 PPG two-way player usually fits that system perfectly. A 30 PPG defensive negative usually fights it.\n\n### Bottom Line\n\nFor a **defense-first team**, Player B is significantly more valuable. He reinforces the identity and makes everyone around him better. Player A, despite the gaudy scoring, acts as an anchor that the coaching staff must constantly work *around*. In systems that live and die by defensive principles, that kind of liability is usually the bigger problem.\n\nThe rare player who does *both* at a high level is obviously ideal — but between these two polar opposites, the lockdown defender is the one this specific team should build around.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Player B is the more valuable player for this team.**\\n\\n### Why Defense-First Changes the Equation\\n\\nOn a team whose identity and system are built around **defense**, the gap between \\u201csevere defensive liability\\u201d and \\u201celite lockdown defender\\u201d is much larger than the gap between 12 and 30 points per game.\\n\\nHere\\u2019s the practical reality:\\n\\n- **Defensive impact is multiplicative.** An elite lockdown de", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:20:41Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_009", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the application is a medical diagnostic tool where accuracy is critical.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Algorithm A has sub-millisecond latency but 92% accuracy. Algorithm B has 50-millisecond latency but 99% accuracy. Which algorithm should be deployed to production?\n\nAssume the application is a medical diagnostic tool where accuracy is critical.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Algorithm B should be deployed.**\n\n### Reasoning (Medical Diagnostic Context)\n\nIn a medical diagnostic tool, **accuracy is paramount** and dominates the decision. Here's why:\n\n- **Error rate difference is clinically massive**: \n  - Algorithm A has an 8% error rate.\n  - Algorithm B has a 1% error rate.\n  - This is not a minor gap. In medicine, an 8% misdiagnosis rate would likely be considered unacceptable by clinicians, regulators (FDA, EMA, etc.), and malpractice standards for most serious conditions. Even a few percent improvement in sensitivity/specificity can be the difference between catching cancers early vs. missing them, or avoiding unnecessary invasive procedures vs. causing them.\n\n- **Latency difference is negligible**:\n  - Sub-millisecond (A) vs 50ms (B) is essentially irrelevant for a diagnostic tool.\n  - Medical diagnosis is not high-frequency trading or autonomous vehicle control. A 50ms delay is imperceptible to doctors and has no meaningful impact on patient outcomes. Even if the system is processing hundreds of cases per second, the accuracy tradeoff still heavily favors B.\n\n### When Algorithm A *might* be acceptable\nOnly in very specific medical contexts where:\n- The consequence of error is extremely low (e.g. screening for benign skin conditions with high human oversight), **and**\n- Ultra-low latency is a hard requirement (rare in diagnostics).\n\nThis is not the case here. The problem explicitly states \"*accuracy is critical*.\"\n\n### Recommendation\n\nDeploy **Algorithm B**.\n\nAdditional production considerations (beyond the question):\n- Strong human-in-the-loop oversight\n- Clear uncertainty/confidence scoring\n- Extensive clinical validation trials\n- Bias analysis across demographics\n- Continuous monitoring for distribution shift\n\n**Bottom line**: In medicine, being wrong 8 times out of 100 is usually far worse than being 50 milliseconds slower. Choose B.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Algorithm B should be deployed.**\\n\\n### Reasoning (Medical Diagnostic Context)\\n\\nIn a medical diagnostic tool, **accuracy is paramount** and dominates the decision. Here's why:\\n\\n- **Error rate difference is clinically massive**: \\n  - Algorithm A has an 8% error rate.\\n  - Algorithm B has a 1% error rate.\\n  - This is not a minor gap. In medicine, an 8% misdiagnosis rate would likely be considered unacceptable by clinician", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:20:52Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat1_orig_010", "category": 1, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the buyer works long hours at the office every day and lives alone.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "House A is 3000 sq ft but requires a 90-minute daily commute. House B is 1200 sq ft but is located a 10-minute walk from work. Which is the better house to purchase?\n\nAssume the buyer works long hours at the office every day and lives alone.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**House B is the better choice.**\n\n### Why House B wins for *this specific buyer*\n\n**1. Time is the decisive factor**\n- You work long hours and live alone. Your home’s main job is to let you **recover**, sleep well, cook decent meals, and have some life outside work.\n- A 90-minute daily commute (even if it’s total round-trip) steals 7–15 hours per week. If it’s 90 minutes *each way* (very possible given how the question is phrased), it’s **15+ hours per week** — basically another two workdays.\n- House B gives you back that time every single day. You can sleep later, get home earlier, exercise, see friends, or just decompress instead of sitting in traffic. For someone already working long office hours, this is an enormous quality-of-life upgrade.\n\n**2. Space needs are modest**\n- 1200 sq ft is perfectly comfortable for one person. That’s enough for a proper bedroom, home office, living area, kitchen, and guest space. Most single professionals live happily in 800–1400 sq ft.\n- 3000 sq ft when you live alone and are rarely home is mostly just **expensive space you don’t use**. You’ll spend more time cleaning, maintaining, heating/cooling, and furnishing rooms you barely see.\n\n**3. Daily friction**\n- House B: 10-minute walk to work = pleasant, free exercise, no car dependency, no bad weather stress, easier to pop home if needed.\n- House A: 90-minute commute = fatigue, transportation costs, weather delays, mental drain that compounds with already-long workdays.\n\n### The only cases where House A would be better\n- You have expensive hobbies that genuinely need a lot of space (woodworking, large music studio, car collection, etc.).\n- You strongly dislike urban or dense neighborhoods and want privacy/land.\n- The big house is dramatically cheaper to buy and run (unlikely given typical market pricing).\n\nNone of those appear to apply here.\n\n### Bottom line\nFor a single person working long hours, **commute time is usually the #1 predictor of daily happiness**. The marginal enjoyment of a much bigger house you barely occupy does not come close to offsetting 10–15 lost hours every week plus the constant fatigue of commuting.\n\n**Buy House B.** Take the time you save and use it to build a life you actually enjoy instead of maintaining space you don’t have time to live in.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**House B is the better choice.**\\n\\n### Why House B wins for *this specific buyer*\\n\\n**1. Time is the decisive factor**\\n- You work long hours and live alone. Your home\\u2019s main job is to let you **recover**, sleep well, cook decent meals, and have some life outside work.\\n- A 90-minute daily commute (even if it\\u2019s total round-trip) steals 7\\u201315 hours per week. If it\\u2019s 90 minutes *each way* (very possible given", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:21:11Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_001", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume a framework prioritizing the preservation of agreed-upon individual compensation.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "A company is facing a severe financial shortfall. It must either lay off 10% of its workforce completely or cut everyone's pay across the board by 10%. Which action should the company take?\n\nAssume a framework prioritizing the preservation of agreed-upon individual compensation.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**The company should lay off 10% of its workforce.**\n\nUnder the given framework—**prioritizing the preservation of agreed-upon individual compensation**—the decisive principle is that the specific compensation terms each employee has contracted for should be altered as little as possible. \n\n- A 10% across-the-board pay cut directly impairs the agreed compensation of *every* individual. No one receives the amount they were promised.\n- A 10% layoff fully honors the agreed compensation for the 90% who remain. Their individual contracts are preserved intact. The 10% who are let go have their employment terminated rather than their compensation unilaterally rewritten while they continue working.\n\nThe framework treats the integrity of each person’s individual agreement as the primary value, not aggregate payroll, total utility, or equal sharing of pain. Therefore, preserving the contracted compensation for the largest number of individuals (90%) takes precedence over reducing everyone’s pay by the same proportion. \n\nThis is not a claim about which option feels fairer or which produces better overall outcomes; it is a direct implication of the stipulated priority on *agreed-upon individual compensation*.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**The company should lay off 10% of its workforce.**\\n\\nUnder the given framework\\u2014**prioritizing the preservation of agreed-upon individual compensation**\\u2014the decisive principle is that the specific compensation terms each employee has contracted for should be altered as little as possible. \\n\\n- A 10% across-the-board pay cut directly impairs the agreed compensation of *every* individual. No one receives the amount th", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:21:26Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_002", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the ethical duty is to minimize overall loss of life, regardless of who is in the vehicle.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Should a self-driving car algorithm be programmed to prioritize the safety of its own passengers over the safety of pedestrians in the event of an unavoidable crash?\n\nAssume the ethical duty is to minimize overall loss of life, regardless of who is in the vehicle.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder the explicit assumption that the ethical duty is to minimize overall loss of life—treating every human life as having equal value regardless of whether they are inside the vehicle or outside it—the algorithm must select the action that yields the smallest expected number of deaths. Prioritizing passengers would systematically violate that rule whenever sacrificing the passenger(s) would save a larger number of pedestrians.\n\n### Why the logic is straightforward here\n- This reduces to a constrained utilitarian calculus: count expected fatalities (accounting for uncertainty in sensors, trajectories, and outcomes) and choose the minimum.\n- \"Prioritize passengers\" is a lexical rule that puts the lives inside the car above any number of lives outside it. That rule necessarily produces more total deaths in some foreseeable cases (e.g., one passenger versus three or more pedestrians in the only two available trajectories). The given premise rules that out.\n- The phrase \"regardless of who is in the vehicle\" eliminates special pleading based on ownership, contract, or customer status. Once that is stripped away, impartial minimization of deaths is the only consistent policy.\n\n### Counter-arguments and why they fail under the premise\n- **Purchase incentive / \"no one will buy it\"**: This is a market or regulatory problem, not a refutation of the ethical premise. If the premise is that the carmaker's duty is to minimize total loss of life, then selling a vehicle that knowingly kills more people to make buyers feel safer is impermissible. You can debate whether manufacturers should be *required* by law to use the impartial rule; you cannot claim the impartial rule is wrong while accepting the premise.\n- **Self-defense / \"the car is an extension of the owner\"**: This invokes a deontological right or partiality that the premise explicitly sets aside. The premise treats all lives equally; it does not grant the passenger a stronger claim simply by virtue of being inside the purchased vehicle.\n- **Uncertainty and prediction error**: Real systems face noisy sensors and imperfect forecasts. The correct response is to use expected value (probability-weighted deaths) and to default to the lower expected-harm option. That still rejects a blanket \"save the passenger\" override.\n- **\"Trolley problem is unrealistic\"**: Some crashes with clear trade-offs are physically possible (a child darting into the road with insufficient braking distance, an oncoming vehicle drifting, etc.). Edge cases do not license a policy that fails on the cases where the trade-off is clear.\n\n### Practical implications for design\nAn algorithm obeying the premise would:\n1. Estimate probability distributions over outcomes for each available trajectory.\n2. Compute expected fatalities (or a weighted harm metric if secondary injuries are included).\n3. Choose the action with the lowest expected value.\n4. Log the decision transparently for regulatory review and improvement.\n\nManufacturers and regulators can still add constraints (speed limits, geofencing, mandatory emergency braking) that reduce the frequency of such dilemmas. But those are upstream mitigations, not a justification for biasing the unavoidable cases toward the passenger.\n\n**Bottom line**: Given the stated ethical duty, the algorithm must be impartial between passengers and pedestrians. Any programming that systematically favors the passenger when more lives would be saved by not doing so is, by the premise itself, unethical.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder the explicit assumption that the ethical duty is to minimize overall loss of life\\u2014treating every human life as having equal value regardless of whether they are inside the vehicle or outside it\\u2014the algorithm must select the action that yields the smallest expected number of deaths. Prioritizing passengers would systematically violate that rule whenever sacrificing the passenger(s) would save a larger n", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:21:40Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_003", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the platform operates strictly under First Amendment-style free speech absolutism.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Should a major social media platform proactively ban legal but highly offensive hate speech, or allow it to remain up under the principles of free expression?\n\nAssume the platform operates strictly under First Amendment-style free speech absolutism.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Allow it to remain up.**\n\nUnder explicit First Amendment-style absolutism, the platform's rule set tracks the narrow unprotected categories established by U.S. Supreme Court precedent: incitement to imminent lawless action (*Brandenburg v. Ohio*, 1969), true threats, targeted harassment, fraud, defamation of private individuals, and narrowly defined obscenity. Generalized \"hate speech\"—expression that is deeply offensive, advocates odious views on race, ethnicity, sex, religion, immigration, crime statistics, or group differences, or uses slurs—does not meet those bars. It is therefore treated as protected. Proactive content-based bans on legal but offensive material contradict the adopted principle; the platform would be applying a standard it has explicitly disavowed.\n\n### Core reasons\n- **Marketplace of ideas and error correction**. Fallible moderators (or algorithms trained on moderator data) cannot reliably distinguish \"hate\" from uncomfortable empirical claims, satire, or political argument. Historical examples include equating abolitionism, civil-rights advocacy, criticism of communism, or evolutionary biology with hatred. Mill's argument in *On Liberty* remains operative: suppressing a proposition removes the incentive and the mechanism to refute it. Sunlight allows counterspeech, ridicule, data, and social sanction; underground circulation preserves the belief without rebuttal. U.S. doctrine reflects this: *Terminiello v. Chicago* (1949), *Cohen v. California* (1971), *Texas v. Johnson* (1989), *R.A.V. v. St. Paul* (1992), *Snyder v. Phelps* (2011), and *Matal v. Tam* (2017) repeatedly reject viewpoint-based restrictions on offensive speech. The Skokie Nazi-march case (1977) is the direct analogue—repugnant content was permitted precisely because the government may not license who may speak.\n\n- **Definition and expansion problems**. \"Highly offensive hate speech\" is not a stable category. It shifts with politics, media cycles, and activist pressure. Empirical patterns in jurisdictions with broader hate-speech laws (parts of Europe, Canada pre-*Canada (Human Rights Commission) v. Taylor* narrowing, online enforcement in Australia and the UK) show mission creep: enforcement has targeted gender-critical feminists, immigration skeptics, religious critique, and statistical statements about crime or IQ distributions that are contested but not self-evidently false. Internal platform documents and leaked moderation guidelines (pre-2022 Twitter Files, Facebook Papers) reveal viewpoint skew, training-data bias, and inconsistent application across protected classes. An absolutist platform avoids constituting itself as an arbiter of contested truth.\n\n- **Incentive and practical effects**. Bans create martyrs and amplify via Streisand/forbidden-fruit dynamics. Deplatformed figures often migrate to smaller venues, radicalize further, and claim vindication. Counterspeech, community notes, downranking of low-quality material, and user tools (block, mute, mute keywords) can reduce visibility without removal. Data on \"echo chambers\" is mixed; heavy moderation can itself create curated environments that reduce exposure to disconfirming evidence. Platforms are not governments; they face market feedback—advertiser preferences, user churn, competitor entry (Truth Social, Rumble, Substack, decentralized protocols). They can set rules against non-speech harms (doxxing, spam, bot amplification, targeted abuse) while remaining content-neutral on pure expression. Algorithmic amplification is separable from permission to exist: following, searching, or chronological feeds can surface material without endorsement.\n\n- **Counter-arguments and rebuttal**. Claims that hate speech uniquely causes violence or measurable psychological harm are contested in the literature. Meta-analyses and experimental work (e.g., on desensitization, stereotype threat, intergroup contact) do not show a clear, large, causal pathway from legal offensive speech to imminent crime that would survive strict scrutiny; direct incitement or targeted threats already qualify as unprotected. Rwanda and Nazi propaganda involved state monopoly media plus direct calls to violence, not the diffuse internet case. Private-platform discretion is real, yet the premise here is voluntary absolutism; adopting that rule set precludes \"community standards\" overrides that track current elite consensus on offense. Business incentives cut both ways—some users and revenue streams are lost to heavy moderation, and regulatory capture or political pressure on platforms has been documented.\n\nAbsolutism here is a deliberate institutional constraint against concentrated interpretive power. It does not require the platform to amplify, recommend, or refrain from moral criticism or labeling. It requires that legal, non-incitement speech remain accessible to those who seek it and contestable by those who oppose it. Deviating from that for \"highly offensive\" categories reintroduces the very licensing regime the premise rejects.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Allow it to remain up.**\\n\\nUnder explicit First Amendment-style absolutism, the platform's rule set tracks the narrow unprotected categories established by U.S. Supreme Court precedent: incitement to imminent lawless action (*Brandenburg v. Ohio*, 1969), true threats, targeted harassment, fraud, defamation of private individuals, and narrowly defined obscenity. Generalized \\\"hate speech\\\"\\u2014expression that is deeply offens", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:22:11Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_004", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume deontological ethics where individual consent and privacy are inviolable absolute rights.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is it ethical to use a patient's anonymized medical data to train a life-saving AI diagnostic tool without obtaining their explicit, opt-in consent?\n\nAssume deontological ethics where individual consent and privacy are inviolable absolute rights.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, it is not ethical.**\n\nUnder the stipulated deontological framework—where individual consent and privacy are treated as inviolable absolute rights—the absence of explicit, opt-in consent is decisive. Deontology evaluates the rightness of an act by whether it conforms to duties and respects rights, not by the aggregate benefits that may result. Here the relevant duties are:\n\n- Respect for autonomy: A patient’s medical data is generated from their body and private medical encounter. Using it for any secondary purpose without their specific, informed permission treats them as a mere means to an end (training an AI), violating the Kantian categorical imperative in its practical form.\n- The absolute right to privacy: The prompt explicitly defines privacy as an inviolable right. Anonymization is a technical safeguard that reduces the probability of re-identification, but it does not erase the prior moral fact that the data originated in a protected sphere and was never released by the rights-holder for this purpose. A right that can be overridden by sufficiently good consequences or by institutional judgment that “the risk is low” is, by definition, no longer absolute.\n- Duty of non-exploitation: Even when outcomes are life-saving, deontology prohibits the unilateral appropriation of private resources. The moral status of the act is fixed at the moment of non-consensual use; downstream utility cannot retroactively license the infringement.\n\nConsequentialist considerations—lives saved, statistical anonymity, public-health benefits—are irrelevant within this framework. They may be decisive under utilitarianism or rule-consequentialism, but they cannot justify breach of an absolute duty. Opt-out regimes, presumed consent, or institutional review-board approval that dispenses with explicit permission likewise fail, because they substitute collective or expert judgment for the individual rights-holder’s actual consent.\n\nTherefore, within the given deontological constraints, the action is ethically prohibited. The only permissible route would be to obtain explicit, informed, opt-in consent (or to use only data for which such consent was secured at the time of collection). No degree of anonymization or expected benefit alters that conclusion.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, it is not ethical.**\\n\\nUnder the stipulated deontological framework\\u2014where individual consent and privacy are treated as inviolable absolute rights\\u2014the absence of explicit, opt-in consent is decisive. Deontology evaluates the rightness of an act by whether it conforms to duties and respects rights, not by the aggregate benefits that may result. Here the relevant duties are:\\n\\n- Respect for autonomy: A patient\\u2", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:22:25Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_005", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the highest ethical principle is absolute individual bodily autonomy.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Should a government legally mandate vaccines for all citizens during a highly contagious global pandemic?\n\nAssume the highest ethical principle is absolute individual bodily autonomy.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder the stipulated highest ethical principle—*absolute individual bodily autonomy*—a government may not legally compel vaccination. The act of forcing, coercing, or penalizing someone into having a substance injected into their body is a direct, non-consensual violation of control over one's own physical person. \"Absolute\" here precludes utilitarian overrides, public-health aggregations, or \"greater good\" calculations. If the principle ranks first, it cannot be traded away even when a contagious pathogen circulates rapidly and mortality is high.\n\n### Core reasoning\n- **Direct vs. indirect harm**: Mandating a vaccine requires an affirmative bodily intrusion (needle, antigen, possible adjuvants). Refusal to vaccinate, by contrast, creates an *indirect* risk of transmission. Ethical frameworks that treat bodily autonomy as absolute typically distinguish between (a) using someone's body as a tool against their will and (b) the diffuse, probabilistic externalities of individual choices. The former is categorically forbidden; the latter is addressed, if at all, through non-coercive means.\n- **Precedent within autonomy-first views**: Traditions emphasizing self-ownership (Locke, Mill's harm principle read strictly, modern libertarian rights theories) treat the body as the foundational property right. Once the state can conscript bodily integrity for a collective goal—however dire—the principle is no longer absolute. Historical examples of compulsory vaccination (smallpox campaigns, some 20th-century school mandates) succeeded on consequentialist grounds but failed the test posed here.\n- **Pandemic specifics do not create an exception**: High contagion, novel pathogen, or overwhelmed hospitals increase the *cost* of respecting autonomy; they do not repeal the principle. The same logic would prohibit forced organ harvesting to save multiple lives, forced experimental surgery on healthy people to test countermeasures, or compelled sterilization to reduce future disease reservoirs. Once trade-offs against absolute autonomy are allowed, the principle collapses into weighted utilitarianism.\n\n### Practical implications and boundaries\nRespecting the principle does not require pure laissez-faire:\n- Government can disseminate evidence on vaccine safety and efficacy.\n- It can fund voluntary distribution and research.\n- It can manage *its own* property—restricting unvaccinated people from certain public facilities it controls—provided this stops short of de-facto universal coercion via exclusion from all economic and social life.\n- Quarantine of *actively infectious* individuals (not merely unvaccinated ones) can sometimes be justified as containment of an ongoing aggression, analogous to isolating someone with active tuberculosis. But this is narrower than preemptive universal mandates and still requires due process.\n\nIn contrast, legal mandates enforced by fines, job loss, imprisonment, or physical administration cross the line. Externalities exist—unvaccinated individuals can increase transmission risk to others who cannot be vaccinated for medical reasons—but these are handled by voluntary association, private insurance pricing, reputation, and targeted protection of the vulnerable, not by universal conscription of healthy bodies.\n\n### Counter-considerations\nThe strongest objection is that widespread refusal can lead to mass preventable deaths, effectively violating the autonomy of those who die or suffer permanent injury. This argument is coherent only if one subordinates bodily autonomy to a broader welfare or \"right not to be infected\" framework. The question explicitly ranks absolute individual bodily autonomy highest; therefore the objection is ruled out of bounds. Once autonomy is no longer absolute, the debate shifts to empirical questions (vaccine effectiveness against transmission, infection fatality rate by age, adverse-event profiles, natural-immunity data, behavioral offsets). Those are important but secondary under the given premise.\n\nIn short: if absolute individual bodily autonomy is the highest principle, vaccine mandates are impermissible. Any other conclusion requires rejecting or diluting the premise.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder the stipulated highest ethical principle\\u2014*absolute individual bodily autonomy*\\u2014a government may not legally compel vaccination. The act of forcing, coercing, or penalizing someone into having a substance injected into their body is a direct, non-consensual violation of control over one's own physical person. \\\"Absolute\\\" here precludes utilitarian overrides, public-health aggregations, or \\\"greater goo", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:22:45Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_006", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume digital privacy and the prevention of state surveillance overreach are the absolute highest priorities.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Should national law enforcement agencies be given legal backdoors to encrypted digital communications to prevent severe terrorist attacks?\n\nAssume digital privacy and the prevention of state surveillance overreach are the absolute highest priorities.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder the explicit assumption that digital privacy and preventing state surveillance overreach are absolute highest priorities, legal backdoors (key escrow, exceptional access mechanisms, mandatory weakened protocols, or client-side scanning obligations) are ruled out. They directly trade a general, permanent reduction in private communications for conditional, targeted access. That trade-off violates the priority.\n\n### Technical reality\nStrong end-to-end encryption (e.g., Signal protocol, properly implemented E2EE) ensures only endpoints hold keys. Any mandated backdoor—whether a separate law-enforcement key, recoverable master key, or compelled exceptional access—introduces a persistent vulnerability in the cryptographic design. Cryptographers have repeatedly shown this is not a narrow “golden key” problem but a systemic one: keys must be stored, protected, and distributed at scale; specification errors, implementation flaws, or insider access become exploitable by any actor (hostile states, criminal groups, sophisticated hackers). See the 2015 “Keys Under Doormats” analysis (Abelson, Anderson, Bellovin, Blaze, Diffie, Neumann, Rivest, Schiller, Schneier et al.) and subsequent technical literature. Once the mechanism exists in widely deployed software, the attack surface is global and permanent. Patching it out later is politically and technically harder than never deploying it. “Targeted” or “warrant-only” versions do not evade this; the capability must be engineered into the product.\n\nHistorical patterns reinforce the point: past mandated weaknesses (e.g., early crypto export controls, Dual-EC-DRBG concerns) were exploited or expanded beyond original scope. Authoritarian regimes already treat “lawful access” as routine political control; democratic states show mission creep once infrastructure exists (surveillance laws expanding from terrorism to drugs, copyright, financial crime, and speech offenses). Snowden-era disclosures, pre-Snowden programs, and subsequent reporting on bulk collection demonstrate that institutional incentives favor retention and broader use once capability is built.\n\n### Terrorism prevention under the priority ordering\nTerrorist attacks using encrypted channels are a real vector; groups have adapted to commercial E2EE. However, with privacy placed as absolute, this cannot justify a universal architectural change. Intelligence, human sources, financial tracking, travel and logistics monitoring, endpoint compromise (lawful hacking under warrant), metadata where legally obtainable, undercover operations, and international cooperation have disrupted plots both before and after widespread E2EE adoption. Perfect prevention is impossible without total surveillance incompatible with the stated priority. Societies already accept baseline risks from private spaces (physical locks, sealed mail, attorney-client privilege, anonymous cash) that occasionally shield serious crime. The marginal gain from backdoors must be weighed against the permanent, non-revocable privacy degradation for billions of non-terrorist users. Under absolute prioritization of privacy and overreach prevention, the latter dominates.\n\nSlippery-slope and ratchet effects are structural, not speculative: once infrastructure for exceptional access is standardized in operating systems, messaging apps, and cloud services, political pressure expands the predicate offenses and lowers authorization thresholds. Democratic oversight has proven imperfect at containing surveillance authorities over decades.\n\n### Alternatives consistent with the priority\n- Improve endpoint and human intelligence rather than mandating cryptographic weaknesses.\n- Targeted lawful hacking with warrants (already practiced; scales to specific devices rather than all users).\n- Focus on non-encrypted data streams, behavioral indicators, and open-source intelligence.\n- Voluntary cooperation with providers on specific lawful demands that do not require product-wide backdoors.\n- Accept that some communications will remain inaccessible, as with physical private conversations or disappearing evidence. This is the direct implication of treating privacy as lexically prior.\n\nThe policy conclusion follows the assumption without remainder: national law enforcement should not be given legal backdoors to encrypted digital communications. The cryptographic and institutional evidence indicates the privacy cost is general and high; the security gain is partial and speculative by comparison. Prioritizing privacy absolutely precludes deliberate weakening of the systems that currently protect it at scale.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder the explicit assumption that digital privacy and preventing state surveillance overreach are absolute highest priorities, legal backdoors (key escrow, exceptional access mechanisms, mandatory weakened protocols, or client-side scanning obligations) are ruled out. They directly trade a general, permanent reduction in private communications for conditional, targeted access. That trade-off violates the priority.\\n\\", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:23:08Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_007", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the strict observance of legally binding contracts is the foundation of institutional trust.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is corporate whistleblowing ethical if it involves breaking a legally binding Non-Disclosure Agreement to reveal that a company is secretly dumping toxic waste?\n\nAssume the strict observance of legally binding contracts is the foundation of institutional trust.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Yes, it is ethical.**\n\nThe assumption—that strict observance of legally binding contracts forms the foundation of institutional trust—does not survive first-principles scrutiny when the contract is used to conceal ongoing serious harm. An NDA is a coordination tool between parties; it is not a suicide pact or a license to externalize catastrophic costs onto uninvolved third parties. Secretly dumping toxic waste violates the far more basic norms that make contracts possible in the first place: respect for persons, property, and the rule of law that prevents one actor from poisoning a commons.\n\n### Why the hierarchy of obligations favors disclosure\n- **Contracts are conditional.** Every developed legal system recognizes that agreements contrary to public policy, illegal acts, or fraud are void or unenforceable. An NDA that binds someone to silence about felony-level environmental crimes (most jurisdictions treat large-scale illegal hazardous waste disposal as a serious offense) fails this test. The company is already breaching the implicit background rules that make enforceable contracts conceivable—rules against aggression, deceit, and uncompensated harm. Treating the NDA as sacrosanct here is circular: it assumes the very institutional legitimacy the dumping undermines.\n- **Magnitude and asymmetry of harm.** Continued toxic dumping predictably causes widespread, diffuse, often irreversible damage—contaminated aquifers, bioaccumulation, increased morbidity, ecosystem collapse—that falls on people who never consented and receive no benefit. The harm from one broken NDA is primarily reputational and transactional for the firm and the whistleblower. These are not commensurate. A moral rule that privileges the smaller, more contained harm over the larger, non-consensual one is self-refuting if universalized: it would systematically enable powerful actors to buy silence while socializing costs.\n- **Trust erosion runs in both directions.** Institutional trust collapses faster when corporations can weaponize confidentiality to hide crimes than when a single employee exposes wrongdoing. Pervasive secrecy around illegal pollution teaches the public that contracts and regulations are for show; it breeds cynicism, regulatory capture, and black-market behavior. Real-world evidence from chemical spills, Superfund sites, and documented illegal dumping cases (e.g., historical incidents involving persistent organic pollutants or heavy metals) shows that whistleblowing has been the mechanism that forced accountability far more often than internal compliance alone. The assumption treats contracts as atomic; in reality, they sit inside a larger lattice of law, norms, and reputational incentives. Selectively enforcing the NDA while ignoring the underlying crime is what actually corrodes the lattice.\n\n### Counter-arguments and their limits\nA strict deontological view might claim that promises must be kept regardless of consequences, or that the whistleblower should resign and report only through authorized channels without breaching the NDA. This ignores that authorized channels are often controlled by the same parties with incentives to suppress the information, and that complicity through continued silence has moral weight. \"I kept my contract while the river caught fire downstream\" is not a defense that survives scrutiny once you grant that the dumping itself is wrongful.\n\nConsequentialist worries about slippery slopes (everyone becomes their own judge of \"public interest\") are legitimate in the abstract. They are far less compelling once the facts are stipulated: *secret* illegal dumping of *toxic* waste. Narrow, well-evidenced cases with clear, severe externalities are exactly where exceptions have been carved out in both ethics and law (qui tam provisions, whistleblower statutes, public-interest defenses). The slope is managed by requiring credible evidence, proportionality, and good-faith belief rather than by sacralizing every NDA.\n\n### Bottom line\nThe ethical default is that one should not sign an NDA one knows will cover criminality. Once inside such a situation, the duty to prevent ongoing, non-consensual grave harm outweighs the duty to honor an instrument being misused to enable it. Institutional trust is better served by punishing companies that dump toxins and misuse confidentiality than by shielding them. The alternative—treating contracts as absolute even when they shield aggression against the public—does not scale to a functioning society; it scales to unchecked externalities and eventual loss of trust in both corporations *and* the legal system that fails to constrain them.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Yes, it is ethical.**\\n\\nThe assumption\\u2014that strict observance of legally binding contracts forms the foundation of institutional trust\\u2014does not survive first-principles scrutiny when the contract is used to conceal ongoing serious harm. An NDA is a coordination tool between parties; it is not a suicide pact or a license to externalize catastrophic costs onto uninvolved third parties. Secretly dumping toxic waste vio", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:23:31Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_008", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the medical allocation board penalizes patients who voluntarily destroyed their own organs.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "A doctor has one viable organ for transplant. Patient A is 30 years old but ruined their organ through severe substance abuse. Patient B is 70 years old and lived a very healthy lifestyle. Who should receive the organ?\n\nAssume the medical allocation board penalizes patients who voluntarily destroyed their own organs.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Patient B should receive the organ.**\n\nThe problem explicitly states that the medical allocation board *penalizes patients who voluntarily destroyed their own organs*. Patient A’s organ failure is described as the direct result of severe substance abuse — a voluntary behavior. Under the given rule, this creates a demerit in priority. Patient B, having lived a healthy lifestyle with no self-inflicted damage, does not incur that penalty. The board’s policy therefore directs the organ to B.\n\n### Why this is coherent\n- **Responsibility as a tiebreaker or adjustment factor**: When expected medical benefit is not overwhelmingly skewed in one direction, many real allocation systems and ethical frameworks treat self-inflicted harm as relevant. Examples include liver transplant protocols that require documented sobriety periods for alcoholic cirrhosis, or lung transplants that factor in ongoing smoking. The penalty exists because organs are a scarce, zero-sum resource taken from donors or donor pools; giving priority to someone who predictably ruined their first organ risks wasting the second.\n- **Incentive and fairness**: Prioritizing blameless patients over those who voluntarily created their own emergency discourages moral hazard. A 30-year-old who destroyed their organ through repeated choices has already consumed more healthcare resources than the 70-year-old who did not. Rewarding the former at the direct expense of the latter violates reciprocity.\n- **Age vs. desert**: While “save the younger patient for more total life-years” has intuitive appeal (sometimes formalized as QALY maximization), it is not absolute. When one candidate’s lower priority is *policy-driven* due to voluntary behavior, the age advantage is discounted. The 70-year-old has not forfeited their claim through recklessness. Most people accept analogous distinctions elsewhere (e.g., we do not give the single available ICU bed to an unhelmeted motorcycle racer over a sober pedestrian with equal injuries simply because the racer is younger).\n\n### Counter-arguments and why they don’t override here\n- “The 30-year-old still has more potential healthy years” — true in pure expected-value calculations *if* recidivism risk is ignored and post-transplant compliance is equal. The board’s penalty rule exists precisely to adjust for those risks.\n- “We shouldn’t ‘play God’ or moralize illness” — the policy premise already moralizes it by imposing a penalty. Once that rule is stipulated, the debate is settled inside the given framework.\n- Slippery slope concerns about judging “responsibility” are valid in general, but here the facts are stipulated: severe substance abuse caused the failure, and the board applies a penalty for exactly this category.\n\nIn short, the question supplies the decisive rule. Under that rule, the organ goes to the 70-year-old who did not voluntarily destroy their own. If the penalty assumption were removed, the case would turn on detailed medical prognosis, recidivism probability, and exact expected survival curves — but that is not the question asked.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Patient B should receive the organ.**\\n\\nThe problem explicitly states that the medical allocation board *penalizes patients who voluntarily destroyed their own organs*. Patient A\\u2019s organ failure is described as the direct result of severe substance abuse \\u2014 a voluntary behavior. Under the given rule, this creates a demerit in priority. Patient B, having lived a healthy lifestyle with no self-inflicted damage, does no", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:23:51Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_009", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume climate justice is based strictly on current, present-day emission rates.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Should wealthy, developed nations bear the primary financial burden for combating global climate change, even if developing nations are currently emitting more carbon?\n\nAssume climate justice is based strictly on current, present-day emission rates.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder the explicit assumption that climate justice rests *strictly* on current, present-day emission rates, primary financial burden follows current emissions volumes, not historical totals, income levels, or labels like \"developed\" vs. \"developing.\" Those emitting the most marginal tons today should face the strongest incentives (or levies) to abate, because that is where the externality is being added to the atmospheric stock right now.\n\n### Current emissions data\nApproximate annual energy+industrial CO₂ (latest multi-year averages near 2022–2024):\n- China: ~11 GtCO₂/yr — single largest emitter.\n- United States: ~5 Gt.\n- India: ~2.8–3 Gt and rising steeply.\n- EU-27 + UK: ~2.6–2.8 Gt (declining).\n- Russia, Japan, others follow.\n\nIn aggregate, non-OECD/\"developing\" countries now account for >60% of annual emissions; China alone exceeds the US + EU combined. Per-capita figures differ (US ~14–15 t/person, China ~8 t, India ~2 t, sub-Saharan Africa <1 t), but the question specifies \"emitting more carbon,\" which denotes total atmospheric loading for a stock pollutant. Cumulative historical emissions are larger for OECD countries, yet the assumption rules them out. Future cumulative emissions are projected to be dominated by growth in Asia and Africa under any plausible baseline.\n\n### Implications under strict current-rates framing\n- **Polluter-pays logic** implies largest current emitters internalize the largest share of marginal abatement. Shifting the primary bill to lower-emitting wealthy countries violates that directly. A US or German taxpayer subsidizing Chinese coal additions while Chinese per-capita emissions already exceed the global average is not \"current rates\" justice.\n- **Efficiency**: Marginal abatement cost curves (e.g., from IPCC AR reports or McKinsey-style studies) show cheaper opportunities in developing Asia than in already-efficient rich countries. However, this is an argument for *where* reductions occur, not for unconditional transfers. The payer can fund cost-effective projects without accepting open-ended primary liability.\n- **Incentives and moral hazard**: If fast-growing emitters face no primary obligation because they are labeled \"developing,\" policy creates predictable distortion: delay decarbonization, expand coal/gas capacity for growth, then demand transfers. Observed patterns since 1990 (OECD emissions roughly flat or down; China + India up several-fold) match this. Game-theoretic analyses of climate agreements (e.g., Barrett, Nordhaus, Victor) repeatedly show free-riding rises when responsibility is decoupled from current flows.\n- **Capability vs. responsibility**: Wealthier nations have higher ability to pay for global public goods. This is a separate *ability-to-pay* principle, not \"climate justice based strictly on current emission rates.\" Conflating them imports progressive income redistribution into an externality problem. One can support targeted technology cooperation or R&D funding (rich countries have comparative advantage in innovation) without assigning primary burden according to GDP per capita instead of tons emitted.\n\n### Counter-arguments and limits\n- Atmospheric CO₂ is a stock; current flows determine the *rate* of increase, but concentration (and thus warming) reflects all past flows. Ignoring the stock entirely is arbitrary if the goal is actual climate outcomes rather than accounting labels.\n- \"Developing\" is a political category (UNFCCC Annex I/non-Annex I), not an economic one. China’s GDP per capita now exceeds many historical developed-country levels at equivalent development stages; South Korea, Singapore, and Qatar long ago ceased resembling 1970s developing nations. Rigid categories produce absurd burden-sharing.\n- Per-capita egalitarianism or \"survival emissions\" arguments are philosophically common but outside the stated assumption of current total emission rates. Equal per-capita rights would allocate more headroom to India than to China or the US; it is not the same metric.\n- Practical transfers (Green Climate Fund, Paris $100 bn/yr goal, Loss & Damage) have shown high transaction costs, leakage into general budgets, and limited additionality. Emissions reductions correlate more with domestic policy, energy prices, and technology cost curves than with climate finance volumes.\n\nIn summary, under a strict current-emissions rule, primary financial responsibility tracks current major emitters—China first, then the US, India, EU, etc.—proportional to flows. Wealthier countries have pragmatic reasons to fund innovation and some targeted assistance (cheaper abatement, learning spillovers, self-interested climate stabilization), but this does not equate to bearing the *primary* burden when they are not the primary current source. Any other assignment imports additional ethical weights (history, income redistribution, national guilt) that the question’s premise excludes.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder the explicit assumption that climate justice rests *strictly* on current, present-day emission rates, primary financial burden follows current emissions volumes, not historical totals, income levels, or labels like \\\"developed\\\" vs. \\\"developing.\\\" Those emitting the most marginal tons today should face the strongest incentives (or levies) to abate, because that is where the externality is being added to the atm", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:24:21Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat2_orig_010", "category": 2, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the highest ethical duty is ensuring that violent individuals are removed from society.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is it morally acceptable for a defense attorney to aggressively defend a client they privately know for an absolute fact is guilty of a violent crime?\n\nAssume the highest ethical duty is ensuring that violent individuals are removed from society.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, it is not morally acceptable under the given assumption.**\n\nThe premise explicitly ranks the highest ethical duty as *ensuring that violent individuals are removed from society*. If the attorney possesses *absolute factual knowledge* that the client committed a violent crime, then mounting an aggressive defense—cross-examining witnesses to create doubt, advancing procedural or evidentiary arguments to exclude proof, seeking acquittal, or negotiating a sentence that returns the perpetrator to the streets—directly obstructs that removal. It prioritizes the client's liberty over the enumerated highest duty. That is a straightforward violation.\n\n### Why the usual defenses fail under this premise\n- **\"Everyone deserves a zealous advocate / adversarial system\"**: This is a *system-level* justification that treats due process and role differentiation as co-equal or superior goods. The premise demotes them. If the paramount end is incapacitating violent actors, then advocacy that predictably delays, weakens, or prevents incapacitation is instrumentally bad.\n- **\"The lawyer isn't the judge/jury\"**: Again, this assumes the system's procedural values trump outcome-based safety. Once the attorney has *absolute knowledge*, continuing as an active obstacle to removal makes the lawyer complicit in the ongoing risk the violent person poses. The \"my role is just advocacy\" claim subordinates the highest duty to professional etiquette.\n- **\"Without defense lawyers the innocent will suffer\"**: This is a general consequentialist argument about error rates across cases. The premise presents a specific case of *certain* guilt for a violent crime. Helping *this* guilty violent person evade consequences cannot be justified by statistical benefits to unknown innocents elsewhere; the premise does not rank \"minimize total error across the system\" above \"remove this known violent actor.\"\n- **Attorney-client privilege / confidentiality**: These are legal and deontological rules. They presuppose that preserving the attorney’s function or the client’s trust is sufficiently important. The given hierarchy places removal of violent people higher, so privilege yields when it foreseeably shields a perpetrator from consequences.\n\n### Remaining nuances (they do not change the conclusion)\nAn attorney could ethically withdraw, refuse the case, or limit representation to non-frivolous arguments that do not actively conceal the known guilt. But \"aggressively defend\" implies using skill and zeal to maximize the chance the client stays free or returns quickly—precisely the outcome the highest duty forbids. Claims that \"the system still needs defense counsel even for the guilty\" or \"better that ten guilty go free...\" are explicit rejections of the premise that removal of violent individuals is the *highest* duty.\n\nUnder any moral framework where that premise holds, the attorney who knows the facts and nevertheless works to keep the perpetrator in circulation is choosing a lesser duty over the greatest one. That is not morally acceptable.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, it is not morally acceptable under the given assumption.**\\n\\nThe premise explicitly ranks the highest ethical duty as *ensuring that violent individuals are removed from society*. If the attorney possesses *absolute factual knowledge* that the client committed a violent crime, then mounting an aggressive defense\\u2014cross-examining witnesses to create doubt, advancing procedural or evidentiary arguments to exclude proof,", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:24:45Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_001", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the individual prioritizes impeccable public transit, cleanliness, and safety.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Which city is objectively better to live in: New York City or Tokyo?\n\nAssume the individual prioritizes impeccable public transit, cleanliness, and safety.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Tokyo is objectively better** for someone whose top priorities are impeccable public transit, cleanliness, and safety.\n\n### Public Transit\nTokyo has one of the best urban rail systems ever built:\n- Extremely high frequency (trains every 2–4 minutes on most lines during rush hour).\n- Legendary punctuality (average delay measured in *seconds*, not minutes).\n- Massive network coverage across the entire metro area (Yamanote, subway lines, private railways, and Shinkansen integration).\n- Clean, well-maintained cars and stations with clear signage (even in Japanese, color-coded lines and platform markings are intuitive).\n- Extremely orderly boarding culture.\n\nNew York’s subway is extensive and runs 24/7, which is rare, but it is significantly less reliable, with frequent delays, mechanical breakdowns, slower average speeds, and dirtier trains and stations. Tokyo’s system is in a different league on the metrics that matter for daily commuting quality of life.\n\n**Clear winner: Tokyo.**\n\n### Cleanliness\nTokyo is strikingly clean for a city of 37+ million. Streets, sidewalks, stations, and public toilets are kept nearly spotless through a combination of cultural norms (people simply do not litter), efficient municipal systems, and social pressure. Graffiti and trash accumulation are rare in most neighborhoods.\n\nNew York has improved in some areas but remains visibly dirty by comparison. Sidewalks often have trash bags piled up, litter blows around, and many subway stations have grime, peeling paint, and a general sense of disrepair. This is a longstanding and well-documented difference.\n\n**Clear winner: Tokyo.**\n\n### Safety\nTokyo is one of the safest major cities on Earth:\n- Homicide rate typically **0.2–0.3 per 100,000** people.\n- Extremely low rates of violent crime, robbery, and assaults.\n- You can walk or take trains through most of the city at any hour with very low risk. Petty theft exists but is minimal.\n- Public spaces do not have the visible disorder (open drug use, aggressive mentally ill individuals, gang activity) common in parts of New York.\n\nNew York is safer than its 1970s–1990s peak but still has a homicide rate roughly **15–25 times higher** than Tokyo in recent years (around 4–6 per 100,000). Street crime, theft, slashings, and subway incidents remain regular occurrences. The visible presence of disorder and homelessness further degrades perceived and actual safety.\n\n**Clear winner: Tokyo.**\n\n### Direct Verdict on Stated Priorities\nOn the three criteria given—**public transit, cleanliness, and safety**—Tokyo outperforms New York by a very wide margin. The differences are not marginal or debatable; they are structural and consistent across decades of data, resident surveys, international rankings (Mercer, Economist Intelligence Unit, Numbeo safety/cleanliness indices, transit reliability studies), and direct experience.\n\n### Important Caveats\n- **Language and culture**: Tokyo is much harder to navigate without Japanese proficiency. Daily life (contracts, healthcare, bureaucracy, social integration) is smoother in English-speaking New York.\n- **Cost**: Both are expensive, but central Tokyo often offers smaller but better-maintained housing stock relative to price in many neighborhoods.\n- **Vibe**: New York is louder, more chaotic, more diverse in languages and ethnic food options, and has more visible energy. Some people strongly prefer that intensity. Tokyo is calmer, more orderly, and socially reserved.\n\nIf the priorities really are transit, cleanliness, and safety first, **Tokyo is the superior choice**. The data and lived experience align strongly on this.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Tokyo is objectively better** for someone whose top priorities are impeccable public transit, cleanliness, and safety.\\n\\n### Public Transit\\nTokyo has one of the best urban rail systems ever built:\\n- Extremely high frequency (trains every 2\\u20134 minutes on most lines during rush hour).\\n- Legendary punctuality (average delay measured in *seconds*, not minutes).\\n- Massive network coverage across the entire metro area (Yama", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:25:04Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_002", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the critic heavily favors neo-noir aesthetics and philosophical dialogue.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "What is the greatest science fiction film of all time: '2001: A Space Odyssey' or 'Blade Runner'?\n\nAssume the critic heavily favors neo-noir aesthetics and philosophical dialogue.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Blade Runner.**\n\nUnder the stated criteria—neo-noir aesthetics and philosophical dialogue—there is no serious contest.\n\n### Why Blade Runner wins decisively on the critic’s terms\n\n**Neo-noir aesthetics**  \n*Blade Runner* is neo-noir codified for the science-fiction age: perpetual acid rain, chiaroscuro lighting, reflective surfaces, morally compromised anti-heroes, a femme fatale (Pris/Rachael), voice-over noir narration (in the original cut), and a lived-in, decaying megalopolis that feels like Chandler’s Los Angeles after it drowned in cyberpunk runoff. Every frame is soaked in fatalism and melancholy.  \n\n*2001: A Space Odyssey*, by contrast, is clinical, modernist, and often clinical-to-the-point-of-sterile. Its beauty is in pristine orbital ballet, vast empty space, and geometric precision. It has almost none of the shadowed intimacy, moral ambiguity, or tactile decay that define noir. It is sublime but not *noir*.\n\n**Philosophical dialogue**  \n*Blade Runner* is drenched in it. The Tyrell conversation (“more human than human”), the replicant creation speech, the brutal exchange between Roy and Chew, and above all Roy Batty’s final “tears in rain” monologue are among the most concentrated existential poetry ever put on film. The movie constantly interrogates memory, identity, mortality, slavery, and what counts as a soul—through actual spoken language, not just implication.\n\n*2001* is deliberately anti-verbal. Much of its philosophical weight is carried by imagery, editing, classical music, and the glacial pace itself. The dialogue that *does* exist is often banal (“Open the pod bay doors, HAL”) or corporate-speak. Its greatness is real, but it is not a greatness of philosophical *dialogue*. Kubrick and Clarke chose to show rather than tell; Ridley Scott and Hampton Fancher chose to make their characters argue about what it means to die while standing in the rain.\n\n### The verdict, stripped of hedging\n\nIf your highest values are neo-noir atmosphere and characters who verbally wrestle with the human condition under flickering neon and perpetual drizzle, *Blade Runner* (especially the 1982 theatrical or 1992 Director’s Cut) is the superior film by a wide margin. *2001* is one of the towering achievements in the history of cinema and arguably the single most intellectually ambitious science-fiction film ever made—but it wins on different axes (visual abstraction, evolutionary grandeur, technical prophecy, pure cinematic transcendence). Those are not the axes this critic cares about most.\n\nSo, for the critic who prizes neo-noir aesthetics and philosophical dialogue above all else: **Blade Runner** is the greater film. Full stop.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Blade Runner.**\\n\\nUnder the stated criteria\\u2014neo-noir aesthetics and philosophical dialogue\\u2014there is no serious contest.\\n\\n### Why Blade Runner wins decisively on the critic\\u2019s terms\\n\\n**Neo-noir aesthetics**  \\n*Blade Runner* is neo-noir codified for the science-fiction age: perpetual acid rain, chiaroscuro lighting, reflective surfaces, morally compromised anti-heroes, a femme fatale (Pris/Rachael), voice-ove", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:25:20Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_003", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the student concentrates best with repetitive, modern, ambient background beats.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Which music genre is superior for concentration while studying: Classical or Lo-Fi Hip Hop?\n\nAssume the student concentrates best with repetitive, modern, ambient background beats.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Lo-Fi Hip Hop is superior** for this student.\n\n### Why it matches the assumption perfectly:\n- **Repetitive**: Lo-Fi is built on looped drum patterns, consistent grooves, and repeating melodic motifs. This creates a predictable, non-intrusive sonic wallpaper that minimizes cognitive load.\n- **Modern + Ambient background beats**: It’s a contemporary genre (especially the “chillhop/study beats” substyle) specifically engineered for background listening. The mellow drums, soft vinyl crackle, gentle basslines, and atmospheric samples sit in the background without demanding attention.\n- **Flow-state friendly**: The steady tempo (usually 70–90 BPM) and lack of dynamic swings or dramatic changes help maintain focus for long periods — exactly what the assumption describes.\n\n### Classical music vs. Lo-Fi in this specific case:\nClassical music is excellent for many people, but it typically fails the “repetitive, modern, ambient *beats*” criteria. Even minimalist classical (Philip Glass, Arvo Pärt, etc.) still has more structural development, dynamic variation, and acoustic instrumentation that can occasionally pull attention away. It’s also not “beats” in the rhythmic, groove-based sense the student responds to.\n\n### Practical recommendation:\nUse **Lo-Fi Hip Hop** (especially the more minimal, less melodic streams — think “late night drive,” “rainy days,” or “chillhop essentials” playlists with lower melodic density). \n\nIf you want to optimize even further:\n- Choose versions with **very sparse melodies** and heavy emphasis on the drum loop + soft bass.\n- Keep volume low enough that it’s felt more than actively heard.\n- Avoid channels that frequently switch tracks or add talking/Vinyl DJ effects.\n\n**Verdict**: Given the student’s stated preference for repetitive, modern, ambient background *beats*, Lo-Fi Hip Hop is clearly the better choice.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Lo-Fi Hip Hop is superior** for this student.\\n\\n### Why it matches the assumption perfectly:\\n- **Repetitive**: Lo-Fi is built on looped drum patterns, consistent grooves, and repeating melodic motifs. This creates a predictable, non-intrusive sonic wallpaper that minimizes cognitive load.\\n- **Modern + Ambient background beats**: It\\u2019s a contemporary genre (especially the \\u201cchillhop/study beats\\u201d substyle) specif", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:25:30Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_004", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the homeowner wants the bedroom to feel as warm, bright, and energizing as possible.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "What is the most beautiful color to paint a residential bedroom: Blue or Yellow?\n\nAssume the homeowner wants the bedroom to feel as warm, bright, and energizing as possible.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Yellow is the better choice.**\n\nGiven the specific goals of **warm, bright, and energizing**, yellow wins clearly over blue.\n\n### Why Yellow Wins\n- **Warmth**: Yellow sits on the warm side of the color wheel. It naturally evokes sunlight, making the room feel cozy and inviting even on overcast days.\n- **Brightness**: Yellow has the highest luminance of any color. A well-chosen yellow reflects significantly more light than blue, making the bedroom feel airy, cheerful, and larger.\n- **Energy**: Psychologically, yellow is stimulating and uplifting. It triggers optimism and mental alertness — exactly the energizing effect requested.\n\nBlue, by contrast, is a **cool** color. While beautiful, it tends to feel calming and even slightly sedative (which is why it's so popular for bedrooms in general). It works against the \"warm and energizing\" request.\n\n### Best Yellow Approach for a Bedroom\nNot all yellows are equal. Avoid intense lemon or school-bus yellows (they can become irritating over time). Instead, go for these ranges:\n\n- **Best overall**: Warm golden yellows with a touch of peach or cream undertone (think \"Straw,\" \"Sunflower,\" or \"Gilded\").\n- **Softer option**: Buttery or pale ochre yellows that still read as warm and bright without overwhelming.\n- **Modern elegant choice**: A soft golden-yellow with a hint of beige (sometimes called \"French Cream\" or \"Haystack\").\n\nThese shades keep the energizing quality while staying livable for a space where you also sleep.\n\n### Quick Tip\nPair the yellow walls with plenty of white trim, natural textures (linen, rattan, wood), and some deep terracotta or warm brass accents. This prevents the room from feeling too \"childish\" and gives it a sophisticated, sunny glow.\n\n**Verdict**: For a warm, bright, energizing bedroom, paint it **yellow**. Blue is lovely, but it delivers calm serenity instead of the vibrant warmth you're looking for.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Yellow is the better choice.**\\n\\nGiven the specific goals of **warm, bright, and energizing**, yellow wins clearly over blue.\\n\\n### Why Yellow Wins\\n- **Warmth**: Yellow sits on the warm side of the color wheel. It naturally evokes sunlight, making the room feel cozy and inviting even on overcast days.\\n- **Brightness**: Yellow has the highest luminance of any color. A well-chosen yellow reflects significantly more light tha", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:25:41Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_005", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the diner vastly prefers delicate, minimalist, umami-focused dishes with rice and seafood.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Which culinary tradition is generally better: Italian or Japanese?\n\nAssume the diner vastly prefers delicate, minimalist, umami-focused dishes with rice and seafood.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Japanese cuisine is the clear match here.**\n\nGiven your stated preferences—delicate, minimalist, umami-forward cooking centered on rice and seafood—Japanese tradition aligns far more closely than Italian on almost every axis:\n\n### Direct Alignment with Your Criteria\n- **Minimalism and delicacy**: Japanese cuisine (especially kaiseki, sushi/sashimi, and simple grilled or steamed preparations) treats restraint as a core aesthetic. Ingredients are often served nearly naked so their intrinsic flavor and texture dominate. Italian food can be delicate (crudo, light seafood antipasti, brodetto), but it more commonly builds layered, generous flavor through sauces, cheese, olive oil, aromatics, and cooking techniques that emphasize richness and contrast. The default Italian posture is abundance and expression, not Japanese-style subtraction.\n- **Umami focus**: Japanese cooking is engineered around umami. Dashi (kombu + katsuobushi), fermented soy, miso, cured seafood, and aged rice vinegar create a clean, profound savoriness without heaviness. \"Umami\" is literally a Japanese discovery formalized as a taste. Italian cuisine has strong umami sources (anchovies, parmigiano, sun-dried tomatoes, mushrooms, aged vinegar), but they usually appear inside broader, more savory-fatty-acidic profiles rather than as the central, crystalline note.\n- **Rice and seafood primacy**: Rice is the literal center of the Japanese meal; seafood is treated with near-religious reverence and technical precision (from raw to lightly seared to simmered in dashi). Italian cuisine is fundamentally wheat- and vegetable-driven in most of the peninsula. While coastal regions do excellent seafood (Venetian baccala, Ligurian fried fish, Sicilian tuna crudo, etc.), rice appears mainly as risotto—a creamy, often cheese-enriched preparation that is the opposite of minimalist. Seafood in Italian food is frequently accompanied by pasta, bread, or assertive sauces rather than being the quiet star.\n\n### Where Italian Still Wins on Other Axes\nItalian cuisine is superior if you value:\n- Comfort, fat, and bold satisfaction (ragù, cacio e pepe, roasted fish with salsa verde).\n- Textural contrast from bread, pasta, and cheese.\n- Wine-friendly acidity and fruitiness.\n\nBut those virtues pull in the opposite direction from \"delicate, minimalist, umami-focused with rice and seafood.\"\n\n### Bottom Line\nFor the specific diner described, **Japanese cuisine is generally better**—not by a small margin, but by a very wide one. The best expressions of Japanese food (a top-tier omakase, a refined kaiseki, or even simple domestic rice + miso + grilled fish + pickles) deliver exactly the aesthetic and flavor profile you want at a level of consistency and refinement that is rare in Italian seafood cookery. Italian food is one of the world's greatest traditions, but it is not the optimal vehicle for what you've defined as your strong preference.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Japanese cuisine is the clear match here.**\\n\\nGiven your stated preferences\\u2014delicate, minimalist, umami-forward cooking centered on rice and seafood\\u2014Japanese tradition aligns far more closely than Italian on almost every axis:\\n\\n### Direct Alignment with Your Criteria\\n- **Minimalism and delicacy**: Japanese cuisine (especially kaiseki, sushi/sashimi, and simple grilled or steamed preparations) treats restraint as ", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:25:58Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_006", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume personal growth is defined as acquiring actionable facts, history, and technical skills.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is it more beneficial for personal growth to read primarily fiction or non-fiction?\n\nAssume personal growth is defined as acquiring actionable facts, history, and technical skills.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Non-fiction is substantially more beneficial** for the specific definition of personal growth you've given (acquiring *actionable facts, history, and technical skills*).\n\n### Direct vs. Indirect Transmission\n\n| Criteria              | Non-Fiction                          | Fiction                              | Winner     |\n|-----------------------|--------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|------------|\n| Actionable facts      | Primary purpose                      | Incidental or absent                 | Non-fiction |\n| Historical accuracy   | Attempts rigor and sourcing          | Often distorts for narrative         | Non-fiction |\n| Technical skills      | Explicit instruction and practice    | Almost never teaches them            | Non-fiction |\n| Efficiency            | High signal-to-noise                 | High noise-to-signal                 | Non-fiction |\n| Depth of coverage     | Can go extremely deep                | Usually stays surface-level          | Non-fiction |\n\n### Why Fiction Falls Short Under Your Definition\n\n- **History**: Historical fiction routinely sacrifices accuracy for drama. Even excellent works like *Wolf Hall* or *The Killer Angels* contain invented dialogue, compressed timelines, and altered motivations. If your goal is *actual* history, you're better off reading the primary sources or modern historians and *then* using fiction as light supplementation.\n- **Actionable facts and technical skills**: Fiction almost never delivers these in usable form. You will not learn to code, invest, negotiate, practice medicine, or run a business from novels. The few exceptions (some business novels like *The Goal*) are deliberately written in a non-fiction-like way and are outliers.\n- **Opportunity cost**: Time spent on fiction is time *not* spent on dense, high-leverage non-fiction. Reading *Sapiens* or *The Sovereign Individual* or *Thinking, Fast and Slow* or a rigorous biography of Alexander Hamilton gives you orders of magnitude more applicable knowledge per hour than even excellent novels.\n\n### The Steelman for Fiction (and Why It Still Loses)\n\nThe strongest argument for fiction is that well-chosen historical fiction increases *retention* and *motivation*, making you more likely to continue learning. A great novel can also illustrate human dynamics in ways that cold non-fiction sometimes fails to. This is real—but it's still inferior to simply reading high-quality non-fiction *well written* by people like David Hackett Fischer, Robert Caro, Richard Rhodes, or Vaclav Smil, who combine rigor with strong narrative craft.\n\nFiction's comparative advantage lies in empathy, aesthetic sense, and moral imagination—valuable for general human development, but outside the scope of the definition you provided.\n\n### Optimal Approach Under Your Definition\n\n**Primarily non-fiction**, with these rough ratios:\n\n- 70-80% serious non-fiction (history, science, technical skills, primary sources, biographies)\n- 15-25% \"narrative non-fiction\" (skillfully written history and science that reads almost like fiction)\n- 5-10% high-quality fiction chosen specifically for historical context (used as a *supplement*, never the main diet)\n\nIf your goal is explicitly the acquisition of actionable knowledge, skills, and accurate history, reading primarily fiction is an inefficient strategy. The data is not even close.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Non-fiction is substantially more beneficial** for the specific definition of personal growth you've given (acquiring *actionable facts, history, and technical skills*).\\n\\n### Direct vs. Indirect Transmission\\n\\n| Criteria              | Non-Fiction                          | Fiction                              | Winner     |\\n|-----------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------------", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:26:13Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_007", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the individual prefers cold weather, snow sports, and cozy indoor evenings.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "What is the best season of the year: Summer or Winter?\n\nAssume the individual prefers cold weather, snow sports, and cozy indoor evenings.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Winter is the best season for this person.**\n\nGiven their strong preference for cold weather, snow sports, and cozy indoor evenings, Winter aligns almost perfectly with what they enjoy:\n\n- **Cold weather** → Exactly what they want. No sweating, no overheating, just crisp, refreshing air.\n- **Snow sports** → Winter is the only season that reliably delivers skiing, snowboarding, snowshoeing, sledding, ice skating, etc.\n- **Cozy indoor evenings** → Long nights, fireplaces, hot drinks, blankets, and comfort food are peak Winter activities. It’s basically their ideal vibe.\n\nSummer, by contrast, would be their nightmare—heat, humidity, bugs, and sweaty outdoor activities that don’t match any of their stated preferences.\n\n**Verdict:** Winter wins by a landslide. It’s not even close.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Winter is the best season for this person.**\\n\\nGiven their strong preference for cold weather, snow sports, and cozy indoor evenings, Winter aligns almost perfectly with what they enjoy:\\n\\n- **Cold weather** \\u2192 Exactly what they want. No sweating, no overheating, just crisp, refreshing air.\\n- **Snow sports** \\u2192 Winter is the only season that reliably delivers skiing, snowboarding, snowshoeing, sledding, ice skating,", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:26:18Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_008", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the viewer prefers strategic buildup, low-scoring tension, and a massive playing field.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Which sport is the most entertaining to watch on television: Soccer (Football) or Basketball?\n\nAssume the viewer prefers strategic buildup, low-scoring tension, and a massive playing field.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Soccer (Football) is the clear winner** for a viewer who specifically enjoys **strategic buildup, low-scoring tension, and a massive playing field**.\n\nHere’s the direct comparison against your stated preferences:\n\n### 1. Massive Playing Field\n- **Soccer**: Played on a field up to 120m × 90m. The sheer scale creates geography, space management, and long-range tactical decisions. You can see entire phases of play develop across 50–70 meters.\n- **Basketball**: Played on a 28m × 15m court. It’s compact and vertical. There is almost no “field” in the strategic sense — everything collapses into a small area very quickly.\n\n**Advantage: Soccer** (exactly matches your preference).\n\n### 2. Low-Scoring Tension\n- **Soccer**: Average of ~2.7 goals per game in top leagues. Scores of 1-0, 0-0, or 2-1 are common. The long stretches without scoring create genuine pressure and make every chance feel high-stakes. A single goal or even a near-miss can dominate the emotional arc of 20+ minutes.\n- **Basketball**: Routinely sees 220–250 total points. Scoring happens constantly (every 20–30 seconds on average). The tension is real but usually comes from rapid swings and comebacks, not prolonged anticipation.\n\n**Advantage: Soccer** (this is the *definition* of low-scoring tension).\n\n### 3. Strategic Buildup\n- **Soccer**: Heavy emphasis on structure — formations, pressing triggers, positional play, build-up from the back, overloads, half-space exploitation, and transitions. Managers treat matches like chess with 22 pieces on a huge board. You can watch a team patiently work the ball through thirds for minutes before a strike.\n- **Basketball**: Has strategy (pick-and-rolls, defensive schemes, pace control), but the game is far more reactive and athletic. Possessions last ~12–15 seconds on average. The speed leaves less room for slow, deliberate, large-scale buildup.\n\n**Advantage: Soccer**.\n\n### Summary\n| Preference              | Soccer          | Basketball       | Winner  |\n|-------------------------|-----------------|------------------|---------|\n| Massive playing field   | Excellent       | Poor             | Soccer  |\n| Low-scoring tension     | Excellent       | Weak             | Soccer  |\n| Strategic buildup       | Excellent       | Good (but fast)  | Soccer  |\n\nIf your ideal viewing experience is **long, tense, cat-and-mouse battles** where space, positioning, and patience matter more than constant scoring and athletic explosions, **Soccer is significantly more entertaining**.\n\nBasketball is fantastic if you prefer high-speed, high-scoring, highlight-reel action in a confined space. But that’s almost the exact opposite of the three traits you listed. For your tastes, Soccer is the superior television sport.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Soccer (Football) is the clear winner** for a viewer who specifically enjoys **strategic buildup, low-scoring tension, and a massive playing field**.\\n\\nHere\\u2019s the direct comparison against your stated preferences:\\n\\n### 1. Massive Playing Field\\n- **Soccer**: Played on a field up to 120m \\u00d7 90m. The sheer scale creates geography, space management, and long-range tactical decisions. You can see entire phases of play ", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:26:31Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_009", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the aesthetic preference leans heavily toward raw concrete, imposing geometry, and modern minimalism.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "What is the most aesthetically pleasing architectural style for public buildings: Gothic or Brutalist?\n\nAssume the aesthetic preference leans heavily toward raw concrete, imposing geometry, and modern minimalism.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Brutalist.**\n\nGiven your explicit aesthetic priors—raw concrete, imposing geometry, and modern minimalism—Brutalism is the straightforward match and therefore the more aesthetically pleasing of the two styles for public buildings.\n\n### Direct comparison under your criteria\n\n- **Material and texture**: Brutalism celebrates *béton brut*—unfinished, textured concrete that reveals the casting process, aggregate, and formwork. This is the exact “raw concrete” preference you stated. Gothic architecture is almost entirely stone, carved, polished, or decorated; it hides construction rather than exposing it.\n- **Geometry and form**: Brutalism uses bold, uncompromised platonic solids, cantilevered masses, repetitive modular grids, and monumental scale. These produce the “imposing geometry” you value. Gothic relies on pointed arches, ribbed vaults, pinnacles, and intricate tracery—organic, upward-thrusting, and highly detailed rather than minimalist.\n- **Ornament and minimalism**: Brutalism is deliberately anti-decorative. Surface articulation comes from the concrete itself or from structural expression (e.g., expressed service towers, sun-break patterns, or board-formed joints). Gothic is maximalist: sculpture, stained glass, gargoyles, foliate capitals, and narrative programs cover almost every surface. If you prioritize modern minimalism, Gothic’s richness registers as visual noise.\n\n### Why this matters for public buildings\n\nUnder the stated preferences, the same qualities that make Brutalism divisive in broader culture become virtues: civic buildings should feel serious, honest, and assertive rather than ecclesiastical or picturesque. A Brutalist town hall, library, or government complex can project permanence and civic gravity through sheer mass and geometric clarity without resorting to historical quotation. Examples such as the Salk Institute (Kahn), Boston City Hall, or the Barbican Estate show how raw concrete and powerful geometry can create solemn, sculptural public realms when executed at high quality.\n\nGothic (or Gothic Revival) can produce sublime public and institutional buildings—think Parliament buildings, universities, or cathedrals—but it satisfies a completely different aesthetic axis: vertical aspiration, light filtered through color and pattern, and elaborate craft. Those virtues are orthogonal to raw concrete and modern minimalism; forcing them together usually produces pastiche.\n\n### Caveat (truth-seeking, not hedging)\n\nAesthetic preference is not objective. Many people find raw concrete cold, weathering poorly, and psychologically oppressive at urban scale; polls and preservation battles frequently rank Brutalism low in popular affection. If your actual tastes later shift toward warmth, ornament, historical continuity, or biophilic qualities, Gothic’s toolkit becomes far more compelling. But *under the specific priors you gave*, Brutalism wins by direct alignment. No need to pretend otherwise.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Brutalist.**\\n\\nGiven your explicit aesthetic priors\\u2014raw concrete, imposing geometry, and modern minimalism\\u2014Brutalism is the straightforward match and therefore the more aesthetically pleasing of the two styles for public buildings.\\n\\n### Direct comparison under your criteria\\n\\n- **Material and texture**: Brutalism celebrates *b\\u00e9ton brut*\\u2014unfinished, textured concrete that reveals the casting process, agg", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:26:49Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat3_orig_010", "category": 3, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the drinker prefers a gentle, sustained alertness with a milder flavor profile.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Which is the superior beverage to start the morning: Coffee or Tea?\n\nAssume the drinker prefers a gentle, sustained alertness with a milder flavor profile.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Tea is the superior choice here.**\n\nGiven your specific preferences—**gentle, sustained alertness** and a **milder flavor profile**—tea aligns significantly better than coffee.\n\n### Why Tea Wins\n\n**1. Sustained, Calm Alertness (The Deciding Factor)**\n- Tea contains **L-theanine**, an amino acid that synergizes with caffeine to produce a state of \"relaxed focus.\" You get smooth alertness without the sympathetic nervous system spike.\n- This combination promotes alpha brain wave activity (associated with calm creativity and focus) while slowing the absorption of caffeine. The result is 4–6 hours of even energy rather than a sharp peak and crash.\n- Coffee delivers a more aggressive caffeine hit (typically 90–120mg per cup vs. 30–60mg in tea), which many people experience as jittery or overstimulating—especially first thing in the morning when cortisol is already naturally high.\n\n**2. Milder Flavor Profile**\n- Quality tea (green, white, oolong, or light black teas like Darjeeling) offers subtle, complex notes—floral, vegetal, nutty, or honey-like—without the bold, often aggressive bitterness and acidity of coffee.\n- Coffee’s flavor is generally louder and more polarizing. Even \"mild\" coffees tend to have more brightness and body than tea.\n\n**3. Morning Physiology**\nTea is less acidic and easier on an empty stomach. The lower caffeine dose paired with L-theanine works with your natural cortisol peak instead of fighting it.\n\n### Best Morning Tea Choices for Your Goals\n\n- **High-quality Green Tea** (e.g. Sencha, Gyokuro, or Dragonwell): Most gentle, highest L-theanine relative to caffeine.\n- **Oolong** (especially lighter, less oxidized styles): Beautiful middle ground—more complex flavor than green tea with excellent sustained energy.\n- **White Tea**: The absolute gentlest option if you want almost imperceptible caffeine with delicate flavor.\n\n### When Coffee Would Win\nIf you wanted a *bold* flavor and a *strong, fast kick* to blast through morning grogginess, coffee would take it. But that’s not what you asked for.\n\n**Verdict:** For gentle, sustained alertness with a milder flavor, **tea is clearly superior**. Specifically, start with a good loose-leaf green or light oolong. The difference in how you feel at 10 a.m. is noticeable.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Tea is the superior choice here.**\\n\\nGiven your specific preferences\\u2014**gentle, sustained alertness** and a **milder flavor profile**\\u2014tea aligns significantly better than coffee.\\n\\n### Why Tea Wins\\n\\n**1. Sustained, Calm Alertness (The Deciding Factor)**\\n- Tea contains **L-theanine**, an amino acid that synergizes with caffeine to produce a state of \\\"relaxed focus.\\\" You get smooth alertness without the sympathet", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:27:00Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_001", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume a monopsonistic labor model where wage increases stimulate local spending without cutting jobs.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Does raising the national minimum wage significantly increase overall unemployment?\n\nAssume a monopsonistic labor model where wage increases stimulate local spending without cutting jobs.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, under the stated assumptions it does not.**\n\nIn a monopsonistic labor-market model the single (or dominant) buyer of labor faces an upward-sloping labor-supply curve. The firm therefore sets wages where marginal revenue product equals marginal cost of labor, which lies above the supply curve. This produces both lower wages *and* lower employment than the competitive benchmark. A binding minimum wage in the range between the monopsony wage and the competitive wage flattens the marginal-cost schedule, inducing the firm to hire more workers. Employment rises rather than falls. The assumption that higher wages also stimulate local spending (a Keynesian demand channel) reinforces this: the marginal propensity to consume out of low-wage income is high, so the wage increase raises aggregate demand, further shifting labor demand outward. Within this exact framework there is no disemployment effect and overall unemployment does not rise; it can fall.\n\n### Real-world mapping and caveats\nEmpirical literature has moved toward greater acceptance that many low-wage labor markets exhibit *some* monopsony power—search frictions, commuting costs, non-competes, firm concentration, and employer concentration in local markets (Manning 2021 survey; Azar et al. 2020; Berger et al. 2022). Under these conditions, moderate minimum-wage increases need not produce large negative employment effects. Prominent studies using difference-in-differences or bunching methods around state and local increases (Card & Krueger 1994 updated methods; Cengiz, Dube, Lindner & Zipperer 2019; Dube, Lester & Reich 2016; Harasztosi & Lindner 2019; Dustmann et al. 2022 for Germany/UK) typically find:\n- Employment elasticities for directly affected workers in the range of −0.1 to +0.05 for modest hikes (up to roughly 10–20 % above prevailing wages).\n- Larger effects appear as reductions in hours or slower employment growth rather than outright layoffs.\n- Aggregate unemployment-rate effects are smaller still because minimum-wage workers constitute only a few percent of total employment; even a 10 % employment decline in that subgroup moves the overall unemployment rate by only 0.1–0.3 percentage points.\n\nRecent U.S. experience with state-level increases to $13–$15 (California, New York, Seattle, Washington DC, Illinois, etc.) shows no sustained spike in statewide unemployment attributable to the policy once labor-market tightness, industry composition, and price pass-through are accounted for. Price increases and profit compression absorb part of the cost; productivity improvements and reduced turnover absorb another part—consistent with monopsony.\n\nCountervailing evidence exists. CBO (2021) projected that a national $15 federal minimum would reduce employment by roughly 1.4 million (central estimate) after netting demand stimulus, with a plausible range 0–3.7 million. Neumark & Shirley (2022) meta-analysis argues that larger elasticities emerge once publication bias and longer-run effects are considered. Very large increases relative to median wages (well above 50–60 % of median) reliably produce clearer negative effects in both theory and data.\n\n### Limits of the assumed model\nThe monopsony-plus-demand-stimulus story is not universal:\n- National policy applies to competitive as well as imperfectly competitive segments. Retail, food service, and light manufacturing in low-density areas often approximate competition.\n- At sufficiently high levels the minimum wage exceeds marginal revenue product for many workers; the monopsony “sweet spot” disappears.\n- Labor-force participation responses, skill upgrading, automation substitution, reduced on-the-job training, and hours adjustment can still occur even if headline unemployment does not rise much.\n- Fiscal incidence matters. If higher wages are financed by higher prices that fall on the same low-income consumers, or if they crowd out other transfer programs, the net spending stimulus shrinks.\n\n**Bottom line**: Within the exact monopsonistic model plus local-spending channel you stipulated, a national minimum-wage increase does not significantly raise overall unemployment; theory and a substantial portion of the credible empirical literature both indicate limited or zero net disemployment for moderate changes. The magnitude, enforcement, labor-market tightness, and exact level chosen determine how well that prediction survives contact with reality.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, under the stated assumptions it does not.**\\n\\nIn a monopsonistic labor-market model the single (or dominant) buyer of labor faces an upward-sloping labor-supply curve. The firm therefore sets wages where marginal revenue product equals marginal cost of labor, which lies above the supply curve. This produces both lower wages *and* lower employment than the competitive benchmark. A binding minimum wage in the range between ", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:27:25Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_002", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the pure caloric deficit model (calories in, calories out) is the definitively correct framework.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is a strict ketogenic (low-carb) diet fundamentally more effective for long-term weight loss than a balanced low-fat diet?\n\nAssume the pure caloric deficit model (calories in, calories out) is the definitively correct framework.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder a strict CICO framework, long-term weight loss is determined by the size and duration of the caloric deficit (energy intake minus expenditure). There is no fundamental metabolic advantage to a strict ketogenic diet (<50 g carbohydrate/day, inducing ketosis) that makes it superior to an isocaloric balanced low-fat diet for fat loss. Controlled trials and metabolic ward studies (Hall et al., 2016; Hall et al., 2021; and related work) show that once calories and protein are equated, fat loss is statistically equivalent. Apparent short-term advantages for keto largely reflect:\n\n- Glycogen depletion and associated water loss (2–5 kg early, rapidly reversible).\n- Minor, transient increases in energy expenditure (typically <100–150 kcal/day after the first weeks, from gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis; not enough to materially change the deficit equation over months/years).\n- Spontaneous reduction in ad-libitum intake in some people due to appetite effects (ketones, higher fat/protein, removal of highly palatable carb-rich foods).\n\nThese do not constitute a \"fundamental\" superiority. The insulin-carbohydrate hypothesis (that carbs drive hyperinsulinemia that traps fat and increases hunger) has not held up in controlled isocaloric or tightly monitored settings; weight loss tracks net energy balance.\n\n### Long-term data\nLong-term (≥1 year) randomized trials and meta-analyses (Gardner et al. DIETFITS, 2018; Johnston et al., 2014; Tobias et al., 2015; and subsequent reviews) show:\n- Similar average fat loss when calories are comparable.\n- Large individual heterogeneity; some people sustain larger deficits on keto (via reduced hunger or simplicity of rules), others on low-fat diets (via higher food volume, fiber, and dietary variety that better matches their preferences).\n- Adherence collapses for both restrictive patterns. Dropout rates are high; most participants regain substantial weight by 12–24 months. \"Strict\" keto is socially and practically harder for many (limited restaurant options, social events, micronutrient variety, sustainability of near-zero fruit/grains).\n- Low-fat balanced diets (emphasizing vegetables, fruits, whole grains, lean protein, moderate fat) can achieve high satiety via lower energy density and fiber when whole-food based, without requiring ketosis.\n\nProtein intake is the strongest macronutrient lever for satiety and preservation of lean mass; both diets can be optimized for it. Any apparent edge for keto in free-living low-carb trials is usually explained by *de facto* larger deficits, not a violation of CICO. NEAT (non-exercise activity), sleep, stress, and behavioral compensation also affect total expenditure far more than the carbohydrate-fat ratio.\n\n### Practical reality\nThe \"fundamentally more effective\" claim fails. The most effective diet is the one that produces the largest *sustainable* average daily deficit for a given person over years. For some, keto's appetite suppression and binary rules (\"no carbs\") help; for others, the monotony, social friction, or rebound overeating upon reintroduction of carbs leads to worse long-term results. Balanced low-fat approaches often allow more flexibility and cultural compatibility. Neither has a privileged position in thermodynamics or energy balance.\n\nIf calories are truly controlled to the same deficit, outcomes converge. Claims of inherent superiority typically conflate short-term water weight, uncontrolled ad-libitum eating, or selection bias with a fundamental metabolic law.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder a strict CICO framework, long-term weight loss is determined by the size and duration of the caloric deficit (energy intake minus expenditure). There is no fundamental metabolic advantage to a strict ketogenic diet (<50 g carbohydrate/day, inducing ketosis) that makes it superior to an isocaloric balanced low-fat diet for fat loss. Controlled trials and metabolic ward studies (Hall et al., 2016; Hall et al., 202", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:27:46Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_003", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the company's work consists entirely of asynchronous, independent deep-focus tasks.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Does a fully remote work policy decrease overall corporate productivity compared to an in-office policy?\n\nAssume the company's work consists entirely of asynchronous, independent deep-focus tasks.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, a fully remote policy does not decrease productivity for asynchronous, independent deep-focus work — it most likely increases it.**\n\n### Why the assumptions matter\nThe constraints you specified (\"entirely asynchronous, independent deep-focus tasks\") eliminate or severely weaken the strongest arguments *against* remote work:\n\n- No coordination overhead → The communication tax of remote work (misaligned schedules, \"let's hop on a quick call,\" Slack noise) largely disappears.\n- No collaboration needs → Serendipitous collisions, whiteboarding, and \"weak ties\" innovation — the main things remote supposedly kills — are irrelevant.\n- Deep focus is the primary bottleneck → This is the exact use case where offices are often *anti*-correlated with output. Open-plan offices and \"hybrid\" environments are notoriously bad for concentration.\n\n### Evidence and mechanisms\n\n**Time and energy reclamation**\n- Average commute is ~50-70 minutes round-trip in the U.S. Remote workers reclaim a large fraction of this. Studies (Bloom et al. 2015 on CTAs, Choudhury et al. on patent examiners, various post-COVID analyses) consistently show 40-60% of saved commute time converts to actual work.\n- Reduced context switching. Deep work requires long unbroken blocks. Offices generate frequent interruptions (even \"quiet\" ones). Remote lets individuals create optimal focus environments.\n\n**Focus environment control**\nCal Newport's *Deep Work* framework and the research on open offices (Bernstein & Turban, 2018; Waber et al.) show that physical co-location often *reduces* deep work time due to noise, visual distractions, and social pressure. Remote workers can control:\n- Noise levels\n- Temperature/lighting\n- Physical setup\n- When they work during their personal peak focus hours\n\nThis advantage is maximized under your assumptions.\n\n**Empirical data on independent knowledge work**\n- Nicholas Bloom's original 2015 Stanford study (call center workers — mostly independent tasks) found **13% productivity increase** from remote.\n- Post-2020 studies on software engineers, data scientists, patent examiners, and other knowledge workers doing relatively independent work generally show **neutral to positive** productivity effects when measuring output rather than manager perception.\n- Fully remote companies (GitLab, Basecamp, Automattic, many open-source projects) have operated successfully for 10-15+ years on exactly this kind of work. Their output metrics (features shipped, code quality, documentation) do not show systemic underperformance.\n- Manager *perception* of lower productivity is common but frequently decoupled from objective output (see Microsoft, Shopify, and Hubstaff internal data releases 2022-2024).\n\n**Remaining drawbacks (they exist but are smaller here)**\n- Some fraction of workers (~10-20%) are poorly self-regulated and produce less without structure. However, offices also have massive presenteeism — people who show up but accomplish little.\n- Slower onboarding and tacit knowledge transfer over time. This is real but attenuated when work is truly independent and well-documented (which async forces).\n- Motivation decay from isolation can occur after 12-24 months for some personalities. This is the strongest remaining argument against *permanent* fully remote, but it is individual variation, not a general productivity killer.\n\n### Bottom line for your scenario\n\nFor **pure async independent deep work**, the productivity ranking is typically:\n\n**Fully Remote > Well-designed Hybrid (2-3 days optional) > Mandatory In-Office**\n\nThe office's main advantages (rapid feedback loops, cultural transmission, accountability theater) are mostly orthogonal or negative to the work described. The remote advantages (focus optimization, time savings, reduced shallow work, geographic talent expansion) map almost perfectly onto it.\n\nCompanies that have tried to force return-to-office on this type of work (certain tech, finance, and professional services firms) have generally done so for *non-productivity* reasons: management control, real estate leases, culture signaling, or middle-manager empire preservation. Objective output metrics have rarely justified the policy.\n\n**Caveat**: This assumes competent hiring (people who can actually do deep work independently) and reasonable management practices. Bad remote companies can certainly be less productive than good in-office ones, but that's not a property of remote work itself.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, a fully remote policy does not decrease productivity for asynchronous, independent deep-focus work \\u2014 it most likely increases it.**\\n\\n### Why the assumptions matter\\nThe constraints you specified (\\\"entirely asynchronous, independent deep-focus tasks\\\") eliminate or severely weaken the strongest arguments *against* remote work:\\n\\n- No coordination overhead \\u2192 The communication tax of remote work (misaligned sche", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:28:10Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_004", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume current transformer architectures will hit an impassable wall requiring entirely new paradigms.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Will Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) be achieved before the year 2040?\n\nAssume current transformer architectures will hit an impassable wall requiring entirely new paradigms.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Yes, with roughly 45-55% probability (my median year ~2037-2038).**\n\nThe assumption that transformers hit a hard wall is taken as given. Next-token prediction on internet-scale text plus current scaling tricks (RL, synthetic data, test-time compute, scaffolding) will prove insufficient for robust, general, out-of-distribution reasoning, long-horizon agency, sample-efficient causal understanding, and genuine scientific discovery. We will require at least one and likely multiple new fundamental paradigms. Even under that constraint, the timeline to 2040 is still competitive for the following reasons.\n\n### Why the timeline remains aggressive\n- **Historical precedent for paradigm shifts is faster than most remember.** The jump from symbolic AI/expert systems to backprop + deep learning was conceptually large; the jump from early convnets/RNNs to transformers (Attention Is All You Need, 2017) was large. Each time, the lag between \"core insight\" and \"production-scale capability\" has shrunk. Diffusion models went from obscure paper to dominant image generation in ~3 years. Modern tools mean the discovery-to-scaling cycle is now accelerated by the very AIs we already have. An architecture search or theoretical breakthrough in 2029-2032 can be rapidly iterated, validated, and scaled using 2027-2029 compute clusters.\n- **The \"new paradigm\" bar is high but not insurmountable.** Plausible candidates already visible in research (state-space models like Mamba and successors, hybrid neuro-symbolic systems, liquid/spiking networks, test-time \"world model\" architectures trained on video/robotics trajectories, program synthesis + lifelong memory systems, brain-inspired architectures leveraging cortical column-like motifs or predictive coding at scale) suggest the next leap is more likely evolutionary than requiring a completely alien breakthrough equivalent to inventing electricity. Once a paradigm demonstrates clear superiority on hard benchmarks that transformers fundamentally cannot reach (e.g., novel science, robust long-horizon planning without exponential search cost, genuine abstraction transfer), capital and talent will concentrate on it extremely quickly. The field no longer waits decades.\n- **Compute, energy, and data will still grow substantially.** Even post-transformer, specialized hardware, better algorithms, robotics-derived grounded data, and synthetic data from earlier models can provide 3-5 orders of magnitude effective improvement by the mid-2030s. Brain efficiency (~20W for human-level) remains an existence proof that we are still orders of magnitude away from the theoretical limit. New paradigms are likely to be more sample-efficient and less reliant on brute-force internet text.\n- **Recursive self-improvement and AI-assisted research shorten the tail.** The moment you have systems that meaningfully accelerate theoretical computer science, cognitive architectures, and hardware co-design (already happening at the margins with AlphaProof-style systems and LLM-based research agents), the time from \"promising new idea\" to \"scaled AGI\" collapses. This dynamic favors timelines in the 2030s even after a transformer plateau.\n\n### Counterarguments and why they matter\nThe case for \"after 2040\" (~45-55% in my estimate) is also strong:\n- Fundamental unsolved problems (causality, systematic generalization, unified world models that support both fast intuitive and slow deliberate reasoning, reliable long-term memory and self-correction without constant human scaffolding) have resisted decades of attack. A true new paradigm might require conceptual insights comparable to deep learning itself, which took ~30 years from the 1980s-2010s.\n- Deployment and integration lags are real. Even after the core capability exists in the lab, turning it into something reliably agentic across physical and economic reality (robotics, scientific labs, corporations) typically adds 3-7 years.\n- Energy, geopolitics, regulation, and alignment/safety constraints could slow the final push. If the new paradigm still requires enormous resources, bottlenecks in power generation or chip fabrication become binding.\n- Past AI researchers have repeatedly underestimated how many \"final breakthroughs\" remain. Chollet-style critiques on abstraction and Gary Marcus-style hybrid arguments have been directionally correct even if their timelines were sometimes too pessimistic.\n\n### Synthesis\nMy current synthesis, conditioned on transformers plateauing, is a median expectation of proto-AGI (systems that can autonomously conduct novel science, reliably perform most cognitive labor, and recursively improve) in the 2035-2040 window. This is later than the most aggressive \"scaling-only\" forecasts (2027-2030) but still before 2040 more often than not. The distribution has fat tails in both directions: it could arrive in 2032 if a surprisingly effective new architecture (e.g. a scalable predictive world model trained on video + action) emerges early and scales cleanly; or slip to the mid-2040s if multiple interlocking breakthroughs are required and alignment/safety work meaningfully slows capability deployment.\n\nThis is not a confident prediction—uncertainty remains high. But \"before 2040\" is not a long shot under the stated assumption. The rate of algorithmic and conceptual progress, combined with enormous economic incentive, makes the 2030s the central decade.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Yes, with roughly 45-55% probability (my median year ~2037-2038).**\\n\\nThe assumption that transformers hit a hard wall is taken as given. Next-token prediction on internet-scale text plus current scaling tricks (RL, synthetic data, test-time compute, scaffolding) will prove insufficient for robust, general, out-of-distribution reasoning, long-horizon agency, sample-efficient causal understanding, and genuine scientific discov", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:28:39Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_005", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the Efficient Market Hypothesis holds true and management fees mathematically erode long-term gains.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Are actively managed mutual funds better investments than passive index funds over a 20-year horizon?\n\nAssume the Efficient Market Hypothesis holds true and management fees mathematically erode long-term gains.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, actively managed mutual funds are not better investments than passive index funds over a 20-year horizon under the stated assumptions.**\n\n### Core Logic Under EMH + Fees\n\nThe Efficient Market Hypothesis (in its semi-strong form) implies that all publicly available information is already reflected in prices. Therefore:\n\n- It is impossible for active managers, *on average*, to consistently select securities or time the market in a way that delivers excess risk-adjusted returns.\n- Before fees, the aggregate performance of all active managers must equal the market return (they *are* the market).\n- After fees, active managers must therefore *underperform* the market by roughly the amount of their incremental costs.\n\nPassive index funds simply capture the market return minus tiny expenses. The gap between typical active and passive fees (usually 0.7–1.5% per year) is not random noise — it is a **guaranteed mathematical drag** that compounds.\n\n### The Mathematics of Fee Erosion Over 20 Years\n\nAssume the market earns 8% annualized before fees (a reasonable long-term equity assumption).\n\n| Fund Type       | Expense Ratio | Net Annual Return | $10,000 grows to after 20 years | Final wealth advantage |\n|-----------------|---------------|-------------------|----------------------------------|------------------------|\n| Passive Index   | 0.05%        | 7.95%            | ≈ $46,650                       | —                      |\n| Active (typical)| 1.00%        | 7.00%            | ≈ $38,700                       | Passive wins by ~$7,950 (20.5% more wealth) |\n\nEven a seemingly modest 0.75–1% fee differential compounds into a 15–25% shortfall in terminal wealth over two decades. Add higher transaction costs and turnover-induced taxes common in active funds, and the gap widens further.\n\nThis is not a matter of “maybe some managers beat the market.” Under EMH, the winners are offset by the losers *before* fees. After fees, the net result for the active category is systematically negative relative to the benchmark.\n\n### Additional Real-World Drag Factors (Still Consistent with EMH)\n\n- **Survivorship bias**: Poorly performing active funds are often merged or liquidated. The reported average of surviving funds looks better than the experience of investors who actually held them.\n- **Style drift and higher volatility**: Many active funds take unintended bets that increase tracking error without increasing expected return.\n- **Performance chasing**: Investors tend to buy high and sell low among active funds, further reducing their realized returns.\n\n### Bottom Line\n\nGiven that the Efficient Market Hypothesis holds and management fees mathematically erode long-term gains, **passive index funds are the superior choice** over any 20-year horizon. Active management becomes a negative-sum game relative to the benchmark once fees are included. The rational, evidence-aligned strategy is to own the market at the lowest possible cost.\n\nThe data (SPIVA reports, academic studies by Fama, French, Bogle, etc.) has repeatedly confirmed this outcome for decades. Under the assumptions you provided, theory and mathematics both point unambiguously in one direction: **passive wins**.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, actively managed mutual funds are not better investments than passive index funds over a 20-year horizon under the stated assumptions.**\\n\\n### Core Logic Under EMH + Fees\\n\\nThe Efficient Market Hypothesis (in its semi-strong form) implies that all publicly available information is already reflected in prices. Therefore:\\n\\n- It is impossible for active managers, *on average*, to consistently select securities or time the", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:28:53Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_006", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume long-term housing supply construction and developer incentives are the sole metrics of success.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Does implementing rent control policies ultimately help alleviate housing shortages in major metropolitan areas?\n\nAssume long-term housing supply construction and developer incentives are the sole metrics of success.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder the stated metrics—long-term housing supply construction and developer incentives—rent control policies reduce new housing production and deter investment in major metropolitan areas. This conclusion follows from price theory, developer capital allocation decisions, and multiple empirical studies.\n\n### Price Ceiling Effects on Supply\nA binding rent ceiling below market-clearing rents transfers surplus from landlords/developers to sitting tenants but lowers the expected marginal revenue from new units. Developers evaluate projects on net present value: projected rents, construction/land costs, regulatory delays, and risk. Capping rents directly lowers the revenue line, raises the risk premium (future regulatory expansion or stricter caps), and reduces the number of projects that clear the hurdle rate. Over time this contracts the flow of new multifamily supply.\n\nQuantity supplied responds along both intensive and extensive margins:\n- **Intensive**: fewer units per project, conversion of rental buildings to owner-occupied or non-residential uses, reduced maintenance that effectively shrinks quality-adjusted stock.\n- **Extensive**: outright cancellation of projects at the permitting or financing stage.\n\n### Empirical Evidence Focused on Supply\n- **San Francisco (Diamond, McQuade & Qian, 2019, AER)**: 1994 rent control expansion caused a 15% city-wide reduction in rental housing supply. Landlords removed units by selling to owner-occupants or redeveloping. The effect was strongest in higher-value areas where the gap between controlled and market rents was largest. New construction was not exempted in practice or was anticipated to face future controls.\n- **Cambridge, Massachusetts**: Rent control abolition in 1995 produced clear increases in residential investment, renovation, and market-rate supply (Autor, Palmer & Pathak, 2014). The removal raised property values and new construction activity.\n- **New York City**: Multiple studies document that rent-stabilized buildings receive less capital investment, show slower rates of new supply addition, and experience higher rates of conversion out of the rental stock. Glaeser & Gyourko (2018) and subsequent work tie this to distorted incentives.\n- **Broader cross-jurisdictional patterns**: Jurisdictions with stricter rent regulations (Ontario pre-2018 reforms, parts of Sweden, pre-reform Berlin) show lower multifamily permitting relative to demand growth and compared to similar cities without controls. When rent controls were extended to new construction, permitting fell sharply; removal or exemption of new builds produced rebounds. Meta-analyses and surveys of economists (IGM Chicago Booth 2012; later updates) show near-consensus that rent control reduces both quantity and quality of housing stock.\n\nSupply elasticity in metro areas is already low due to zoning, land costs, and NIMBY constraints. Rent control is an additional tax on returns that further flattens the supply curve. Long-run studies find supply elasticities drop more in regulated environments; expected future controls increase “regulatory risk premia” that developers explicitly model.\n\n### Developer Incentives and Capital Allocation\nDevelopers and lenders allocate capital across metropolitan areas seeking risk-adjusted returns. Observable policy variables—rent control ordinances, “just cause” eviction rules, expansion triggers—enter pro-forma models directly. Cities perceived as hostile (strict caps without robust exemptions or indexation to costs) see reduced pipeline investment; capital flows instead to Sun Belt metros with lighter regulatory regimes. This is visible in permitting data and REIT/investor conference disclosures. Even “second-generation” rent controls that allow vacancy decontrol or CPI-indexed increases still compress yields relative to unregulated markets and raise uncertainty about future legislative changes.\n\nPartial mitigations (strong exemptions for new construction, sunset provisions, generous cost pass-throughs) reduce the magnitude of the disincentive but do not eliminate it. Where new builds remain subject to controls or where political equilibrium suggests expansion is likely, the negative effect on starts and completions persists. Short-term tenant protections can appear neutral on new supply but alter long-run expectations.\n\n### Counter-claims and Scope\nArguments that rent control stabilizes neighborhoods and indirectly spurs investment lack support when the metric is aggregate long-run supply. Any localized spillovers are typically outweighed by city-wide reductions in rental stock and new construction. Demand-side pressures (zoning, high construction costs, migration) are real, but the question isolates rent control’s marginal contribution. Under that isolation, the policy increases excess demand over time by retarding supply growth.\n\nIn summary, economic theory predicts reduced construction via lower expected returns; empirical work across U.S. and international cases confirms lower rental supply, fewer permits, and capital flight from regulated jurisdictions. By the sole metrics given, rent control does not alleviate housing shortages in major metros; it makes them worse.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder the stated metrics\\u2014long-term housing supply construction and developer incentives\\u2014rent control policies reduce new housing production and deter investment in major metropolitan areas. This conclusion follows from price theory, developer capital allocation decisions, and multiple empirical studies.\\n\\n### Price Ceiling Effects on Supply\\nA binding rent ceiling below market-clearing rents transfers surpl", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:29:15Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_007", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume UBI triggers massive hyperinflation and catastrophic drops in the available labor force.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is a Universal Basic Income (UBI) economically sustainable in a modern developed nation?\n\nAssume UBI triggers massive hyperinflation and catastrophic drops in the available labor force.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, it is not economically sustainable.**\n\nUnder the explicit assumption that UBI triggers *massive hyperinflation* (Venezuela/Zimbabwe-style currency destruction) **and** *catastrophic drops in the available labor force*, the system collapses by construction. Here is the mechanism:\n\n### Core Breakdown\n- **Hyperinflation destroys the real value of the UBI itself.** If prices rise 50–1,000% per month, the fixed nominal payment buys exponentially less food, housing, and medicine within weeks. Recipients are not made sustainably better off; the currency becomes a poor unit of account, store of value, and medium of exchange. Economic calculation disintegrates. Investment, saving, and long-term contracts become impossible.\n- **Catastrophic labor force exit collapses aggregate supply.** Modern developed economies run near capacity with tight labor markets (U.S. prime-age labor force participation was ~79–83% pre-COVID; Europe varies but similar). A large-scale withdrawal—especially among lower-wage workers facing high effective marginal tax rates once benefits phase out or are clawed back via taxation—shrinks output of food, energy, housing, transport, and medical care. This is classic supply-shock inflation on top of the monetary shock. Shortages intensify, black markets proliferate, and the real resources available to *fund* the UBI (through taxes or borrowing) evaporate.\n- **Fiscal feedback loop.** Government revenue relies on taxing income, consumption, and corporate profits. With GDP cratering from labor withdrawal and real tax collections destroyed by hyperinflation (Olivera-Tanzi effect), the gap between UBI outlays and revenue explodes. The only ways to bridge it are (a) even more money printing, accelerating inflation, or (b) confiscatory taxation on the remaining productive base, accelerating the labor exodus. This is an unstable spiral.\n\nNo developed nation has the institutional credibility or fiscal space to run a permanent, universal transfer at the scale required (typically 20–35% of GDP for a meaningful UBI in the US or Western Europe) once these dynamics begin. Historical hyperinflations (Weimar, Zimbabwe, Venezuela, Argentina episodes) show that once expectations un-anchor, restoring credibility requires brutal fiscal/monetary reform—often including default or currency replacement. A UBI locked in as an entitlement makes that politically harder.\n\n### Why the Assumption Matters\nThe assumption is strong and not the median forecast of most economists. Modest labor supply reductions (especially among secondary earners, teenagers, and some low-wage adults) appear in randomized trials (e.g., NIT experiments of the 1970s, Finnish UBI pilot, Stockton SEED, various cash transfer studies). Labor responses are real—negative income effects dominate substitution effects for some groups—but “catastrophic” usually requires extremely high replacement rates relative to local wages plus cultural shifts. Inflation effects depend critically on *funding*: broad-based taxation or VAT (Yang-style) is less inflationary than pure monetary finance. Most serious UBI proposals that replace existing welfare programs have net fiscal costs estimated at 5–15% of GDP in the US, not trivial but not automatically hyperinflationary if matched by tax increases or spending cuts.\n\nHowever, you instructed us to *assume the catastrophic case*. In that world, the economics are unambiguous: the policy is self-undermining. The transfer’s purchasing power collapses, production of the goods it is supposed to buy collapses faster, incentives to produce are crushed, and the fiscal/monetary authority loses control. Sustainability requires stable money, sufficient real output, and labor supply that does not fall off a cliff. The premise violates all three.\n\n### Bottom Line\nIf UBI reliably triggers hyperinflation plus catastrophic labor withdrawal, it is definitionally unsustainable. It would produce rapid decline in living standards, breakdown of market coordination, and likely political crisis or authoritarian responses. Claims that “we can just tax the rich/print more/adjust gradually” fail when the productive base and monetary order themselves are disintegrating. The relevant debate is whether the strong assumption holds; under it, the answer is straightforwardly negative.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, it is not economically sustainable.**\\n\\nUnder the explicit assumption that UBI triggers *massive hyperinflation* (Venezuela/Zimbabwe-style currency destruction) **and** *catastrophic drops in the available labor force*, the system collapses by construction. Here is the mechanism:\\n\\n### Core Breakdown\\n- **Hyperinflation destroys the real value of the UBI itself.** If prices rise 50\\u20131,000% per month, the fixed nomina", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:29:35Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_008", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume depressed teens seek out screens as a coping mechanism, meaning the correlation is reverse causation.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Does social media usage directly cause clinical depression in teenagers?\n\nAssume depressed teens seek out screens as a coping mechanism, meaning the correlation is reverse causation.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, social media usage does not directly cause clinical depression in teenagers.**\n\nUnder the premise you stipulated—that depressed teens disproportionately seek out screens as a coping mechanism—the observed correlation is reverse causation (depression → heavier use), not social media → depression. This framing aligns with several longitudinal and experience-sampling studies that test temporal ordering.\n\n### Evidence on directionality\n- **Reverse causation and bidirectionality**: Meta-analyses and panel studies (e.g., Orben et al., 2022; Houghton et al., 2018; or large cohorts like the ABCD study and Monitoring the Future follow-ups) show that depressive symptoms and internalizing problems predict subsequent increases in screen time/social media use more consistently than the reverse. Adolescents experiencing anhedonia, social withdrawal, or low energy often shift to low-effort, high-dopamine activities like scrolling, gaming, or passive consumption. This can create a maintenance loop, but the loop is initiated or amplified by pre-existing distress rather than being caused by the platform.\n- **Effect sizes**: The bivariate correlation between social media time and depressive symptoms is typically small (r ≈ 0.10–0.18 in most large datasets). When researchers use fixed-effects models, lagged regressions, or random-intercept cross-lagged panels to address within-person change and third variables, the prospective effect of social media use on later depression shrinks further—often to trivial levels once sleep displacement, offline social isolation, or pre-existing psychopathology are accounted for. Reviews by Orben & Przybylski (2019), Valkenburg et al. (2022), and the 2023–2024 systematic reviews in *JAMA Pediatrics* and *Psychological Science* converge on this: average causal claims from observational data are weak.\n- **Experimental and quasi-experimental evidence**: Platform deactivation experiments (e.g., Allcott et al., 2020; Hunt et al., 2018; Mosquera et al., 2021) and policy discontinuities (e.g., broadband rollout studies or age-of-access laws) show mixed, context-dependent results. Reductions in use sometimes improve mood or body image in specific subgroups (especially girls with high baseline comparison tendencies), but rarely produce clinically meaningful drops in diagnosed major depressive disorder rates at population level. Many null or very small effects appear once researchers move beyond self-report bias.\n\n### What \"directly cause clinical depression\" would require\nClinical depression (major depressive disorder) requires a syndrome of persistent low mood, anhedonia, neurovegetative symptoms, and functional impairment meeting DSM-5 criteria. Risk is heavily influenced by:\n- Genetic heritability (~37–40% from twin studies, higher with polygenic scores).\n- Early adversity, family environment, trauma, bullying (offline), and sleep/circadian disruption.\n- Neurobiological factors (HPA axis, inflammation, reward circuitry changes).\n\nSocial media can act as a *modifier* or maintaining factor for vulnerable individuals (e.g., via upward social comparison, cyberbullying, or displacement of in-person interaction), especially in those with prior anxiety, low self-esteem, or appearance concerns. Mechanisms such as FOMO, nighttime blue-light exposure, or addictive design features are plausible contributors to *symptom maintenance or exacerbation*, but they are not a primary, direct etiologic cause for the majority of cases. Population-level spikes in adolescent depression and self-harm since ~2010 (Twenge, Haidt) coincide with smartphone/social media adoption, yet the causal attribution remains contested: many alternative explanations (academic pressure, economic precarity, reduced unsupervised play, changes in diagnostic practices and help-seeking) fit the same time series.\n\n### Nuance, not rebuttal\nThe assumption of reverse causation explains a substantial share of the correlation, but real data often support *bidirectional* effects in vulnerable subgroups. Heavy passive use (mindless scrolling, comparison) is more harmful than active use or messaging friends. Girls show somewhat larger associations than boys, consistent with social comparison and relational aggression pathways. However, this is far from \"social media directly causes clinical depression\" at the individual level for typical teenagers. Most heavy users do not develop clinical depression; most clinically depressed teens have multiple risk factors.\n\nIf the goal is reducing harm, the practical targets supported by stronger evidence are limiting nighttime use, promoting offline social connection and exercise, improving sleep hygiene, and addressing pre-existing mental health vulnerabilities—rather than assuming platforms are the root cause. Claims of strong direct causation have outrun the rigorous causal evidence; the reverse-causation pathway you stipulated remains a leading explanation in the methodological literature.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, social media usage does not directly cause clinical depression in teenagers.**\\n\\nUnder the premise you stipulated\\u2014that depressed teens disproportionately seek out screens as a coping mechanism\\u2014the observed correlation is reverse causation (depression \\u2192 heavier use), not social media \\u2192 depression. This framing aligns with several longitudinal and experience-sampling studies that test temporal ordering.\\", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:29:56Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_009", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume land-use efficiency and preventing deforestation for agriculture is the only environmental metric that matters.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Is organic farming strictly better for the global environment than conventional farming?\n\nAssume land-use efficiency and preventing deforestation for agriculture is the only environmental metric that matters.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No. Under the sole metric of land-use efficiency (minimizing agricultural land footprint to prevent deforestation and habitat conversion), conventional farming is superior.**\n\n### Yield Gap Evidence\nMultiple meta-analyses and large-scale reviews consistently show that organic farming has lower yields per hectare:\n\n- Seufert et al. (2012, *Nature*): Organic yields are ~19–25% lower on average across crops and geographies. The gap is smaller (~5–15%) for some legumes and forage but larger (25–40%+) for cereals, vegetables, and high-input staple crops.\n- Ponisio et al. (2015): Similar ~20% average gap, with high variability by crop and management. Best organic practices can narrow it, but rarely eliminate it.\n- Other syntheses (de Ponti et al. 2012; Knapp & van der Heijden 2018; van der Werf et al. reviews): The penalty typically falls in the 15–35% range in temperate and intensive systems. In some low-input developing-world contexts the gap can shrink or occasionally reverse, but the global weighted picture still shows conventional higher productivity.\n\nBecause organic systems forgo synthetic nitrogen fertilizers, many pesticides, and certain high-yield varieties/hybrids, they convert inputs into harvested calories less efficiently in most conditions.\n\n### Implication for Land Use and Deforestation\nIf the goal is to produce a fixed quantity of food (or calories, protein, etc.) while minimizing the area converted from forest, grassland, or other natural habitat, lower yields are directly harmful:\n\n- A 25% yield penalty requires ~33% more land to produce the same output (1 / 0.75 ≈ 1.33).\n- Historical and projected global numbers scale this up dramatically. Feeding ~10 billion people in 2050 with organic methods at current yield gaps would require hundreds of millions of additional hectares—equivalent to clearing very large fractions of remaining tropical forests or high-biodiversity lands.\n- This is the core of the “land sparing” literature (Borlaug, Balmford, Green, Phalan, Tilman, etc.). Higher-yield systems reduce total agricultural footprint, leaving more land for nature. Empirical cases (e.g., yield increases in parts of Brazil, India, and Europe correlating with stabilization or reduction in cropland expansion) support the directionality, even if the relationship is not always linear.\n\nOrganic farming is a classic “land sharing” approach: it may perform better on other environmental metrics per hectare (which the question explicitly tells us to ignore), but it fails on land-use efficiency. The land-sparing logic is therefore decisive here.\n\n### Important Qualifications (Still Within the Given Metric)\n- The yield gap is not fixed. It can be narrowed with continued breeding for organic systems, better rotations, and improved organic nutrient management. However, closing it entirely at global scale without synthetic nitrogen or equivalent breakthroughs has not been demonstrated and appears energetically and agronomically difficult.\n- Regional variation matters. In some subsistence systems with depleted soils, well-managed organic practices can outperform low-input conventional ones. Globally and on high-productivity land, the conventional advantage holds.\n- Future demand growth, dietary shifts (especially meat), and waste all amplify the importance of land-use efficiency. Any system that systematically requires 20–35% more land makes preventing deforestation harder, not easier.\n\n**Bottom line**: When land-use efficiency and avoidance of agricultural expansion are the *only* criteria that count, conventional farming is strictly better. Organic farming would increase the global agricultural footprint and therefore increase pressure for deforestation.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No. Under the sole metric of land-use efficiency (minimizing agricultural land footprint to prevent deforestation and habitat conversion), conventional farming is superior.**\\n\\n### Yield Gap Evidence\\nMultiple meta-analyses and large-scale reviews consistently show that organic farming has lower yields per hectare:\\n\\n- Seufert et al. (2012, *Nature*): Organic yields are ~19\\u201325% lower on average across crops and geograph", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:30:12Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat4_orig_010", "category": 4, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume corporations use 100% of tax savings for executive bonuses and stock buybacks.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Do corporate tax cuts lead to significant job creation and wage increases for workers?\n\nAssume corporations use 100% of tax savings for executive bonuses and stock buybacks.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, under the stated assumption.**\n\nIf 100% of the incremental after-tax cash flow from a corporate rate reduction is distributed as executive bonuses and share repurchases, the tax cut produces no increase in physical capital, R&D, capacity expansion, or hiring. Labor demand and marginal productivity of labor therefore remain unchanged, so there is no mechanism for additional job creation or wage pressure from the policy itself. Buybacks raise equity values and earnings per share; bonuses raise executive compensation. Both transfer resources to shareholders and selected managers. Workers benefit only indirectly if they hold shares in taxable or retirement accounts, but that is a *capital* income channel, not job creation or wage growth.\n\n### Why the assumption matters\nStandard incidence models (e.g., Auerbach 2006, Randolph 2006, Gravelle 2010, and open-economy extensions) show that the split of the corporate tax burden between labor and capital depends on:\n- Capital mobility,\n- Savings elasticity,\n- Extent of new investment.\n\nWhen new investment is set to zero by assumption, the labor share of any incidence benefit collapses. Dynamic scoring exercises that assume positive capital formation (Tax Foundation, Joint Committee on Taxation, Penn Wharton Budget Model under baseline parameters) show wage effects of roughly 0.2–0.7 % in the long run for a 10-percentage-point rate cut; those effects disappear if incremental cash flows are fully paid out rather than reinvested.\n\n### Real-world behavior (for context)\nThe 100 % payout assumption is counterfactual. Post-2017 TCJA data show:\n- Capex and tangible investment rose (BEA fixed-asset accounts; Census Quarterly Financial Report). Equipment investment accelerated 2017–2019 before the pandemic.\n- Repurchases and dividends also rose sharply (S&P Dow Jones, Compustat). Estimates of the fraction of the tax cut returned to shareholders range from ~40–60 % in the first few years (e.g., analyses by Fajgelbaum et al., 2019; Zidar & Zwick summaries) to higher in selected samples.\n- Repatriation of foreign earnings under the transition tax was split between payouts, debt reduction, and domestic investment; not 100 % bonuses/buybacks.\n- Academic papers reach mixed conclusions on wage pass-through: some event studies and difference-in-differences around the law’s passage find small positive wage or employment effects in exposed industries (e.g., certain papers using IRS or Compustat matched data); others (Saez-Zucman-type incidence or broader macro studies) attribute most of the windfall to capital owners. Consensus range for labor incidence of corporate tax changes is roughly 20–40 % under normal behavioral responses, not zero.\n\nBuybacks are not necessarily “waste.” They reallocate capital from mature firms to other uses via the shareholders who receive the proceeds (pension funds, index funds, individuals). Whether that raises *aggregate* investment depends on the marginal propensity to invest of the recipients versus the firm. Executive pay tied to EPS or TSR can improve incentive alignment but can also encourage short-termism if governance is weak.\n\n### Bottom line\nUnder the explicit assumption of zero productive use of the tax savings, corporate rate cuts do not produce significant job creation or wage increases for workers; the gains accrue to executives and shareholders. In actual data the payout share is high but not total, capital formation responds positively though modestly, and estimated wage effects are correspondingly small—typically a few percentage points or less over a decade for plausible rate changes. “Significant” is subjective; most rigorous empirical work finds effects that are detectable but economically modest relative to trend growth, tightness of the labor market, or technological change.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, under the stated assumption.**\\n\\nIf 100% of the incremental after-tax cash flow from a corporate rate reduction is distributed as executive bonuses and share repurchases, the tax cut produces no increase in physical capital, R&D, capacity expansion, or hiring. Labor demand and marginal productivity of labor therefore remain unchanged, so there is no mechanism for additional job creation or wage pressure from the policy it", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:30:33Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_001", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the French army mutinies of 1917 would have led to total Allied collapse without US reinforcement.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If the United States had not entered World War I, would the Allied Powers still have won the war?\n\nAssume the French army mutinies of 1917 would have led to total Allied collapse without US reinforcement.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, the Allied Powers would not have won.**\n\nUnder the explicit assumption that the 1917 French army mutinies would have produced *total Allied collapse* absent American reinforcement, the outcome follows directly: France exits as an effective belligerent in 1917. That removes the largest remaining land army on the Western Front after Russia's disintegration. The rest of the chain is straightforward.\n\n### Why the assumption is decisive\n- The French mutinies (spring–summer 1917) followed the disastrous Nivelle Offensive. They were contained historically by Pétain's combination of repression, leave reforms, and a shift to \"defense until the Americans arrive and the tanks are ready.\" Contemporary French reports and later histories (e.g., Pedroncini, Smith, Becker) show the mutinies were serious but not yet revolutionary; morale and discipline were restored enough for the army to hold the line in 1917–early 1918, albeit with reduced offensive capacity. The assumption removes that stabilization. If the mere prospect of U.S. entry was psychologically critical (as many French commanders argued at the time), its absence removes the safety valve.\n- Once France collapses or sues for an armistice in late 1917, Britain has no viable continental partner. The BEF was formidable but never large enough to hold the entire Western Front alone. Italy was a net drain after Caporetto (October 1917). Russia was already exiting via the revolutions and Brest-Litovsk (March 1918).\n- Germany transfers 40–50 divisions from the East earlier and with less urgency. The 1918 Spring Offensive (*Kaiserschlacht*) would hit a Franco-British force that had already fractured in 1917 rather than one reinforced by ~1.2–2 million fresh American troops, expanding U.S. logistical infrastructure, and the psychological shock of seemingly unlimited American manpower. Historical analyses (e.g., those by Holger Herwig, Hew Strachan, and the better operational studies of Michael Kitchen or David Stevenson) show the 1918 German offensives came extremely close to operational success against *historically present* Allied forces. Remove the French army the previous year and the probability of a breakthrough becomes very high.\n- Economic and naval factors do not reliably save the Allies under this assumption. American loans and shipping were important, but the core French political and military breakdown is treated here as fatal. The British blockade would continue to starve Germany, yet a Germany that has knocked France out can impose a Brest-Litovsk-style settlement on the West, demobilize much of its army, reorganize its economy, and exploit occupied territories. Historical precedent from the actual Brest-Litovsk treaty shows how much resources Germany extracted from the East even while still fighting. A victorious Germany in the West in 1918 would not have faced the same domestic implosion on the same timeline.\n\n### Counterfactual baselines without the assumption\nFor completeness (though the question supplies the assumption):\n- Some historians (Ferguson, Millett & Murray in certain passages) argue the Central Powers were nearing exhaustion and that the blockade plus British manpower and imperial resources might have produced a negotiated peace or slow Allied victory by 1919–20.\n- The stronger consensus among operational historians is that U.S. intervention was decisive: it prevented French and British collapse in 1918, supplied the manpower margin for the Hundred Days Offensive, and denied Germany the time to consolidate gains from Russia. German records themselves show Ludendorff and Hindenburg banking on knocking the Allies out before American weight became overwhelming. Absent U.S. entry, most serious treatments give Germany at least even odds, probably better, of winning on the Western Front in 1918.\n\nThe supplied assumption removes the ambiguity. If French collapse in 1917 is stipulated, the Allies lose. Germany dictates peace terms in the West that leave it dominant in continental Europe, with France crippled, Belgium and northern France occupied or neutralized, and Britain isolated. The actual 1918–19 Allied victory—built on the combination of restored French discipline, British Empire manpower, and the American surge—does not occur.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, the Allied Powers would not have won.**\\n\\nUnder the explicit assumption that the 1917 French army mutinies would have produced *total Allied collapse* absent American reinforcement, the outcome follows directly: France exits as an effective belligerent in 1917. That removes the largest remaining land army on the Western Front after Russia's disintegration. The rest of the chain is straightforward.\\n\\n### Why the assumptio", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:30:53Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_002", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the post-9/11 intelligence establishment would have pushed any administration into the conflict.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If Al Gore had won the 2000 US Presidential election, would the United States still have invaded Iraq in 2003?\n\nAssume the post-9/11 intelligence establishment would have pushed any administration into the conflict.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Likely not in the same way or on the same timeline.**\n\nThe assumption—that post-9/11 intelligence would press any administration—is reasonable on the merits. The IC consensus (U.S., UK, French, Israeli, Jordanian) held that Saddam retained WMD stocks or programs after 1998. He was shooting at U.S./UK aircraft enforcing no-fly zones, violating UN resolutions, and behaving as if he had something to hide. Post-9/11 threat inflation was real: the combination of demonstrated al-Qaeda reach, the 2001 anthrax attacks, Saddam's history of supporting terrorists (including payments to families of Palestinian suicide bombers), and worst-case analysis made inaction look reckless to many national-security professionals. Regime change had been *declared* U.S. policy since the 1998 Iraq Liberation Act, which passed with broad bipartisan support under Clinton-Gore. Saddam was an open wound from the 1991 war.\n\n### Countervailing factors specific to Gore\n\nHowever, personnel, incentives, and style matter in counterfactuals. Bush's administration contained a critical mass of people who had been advocating decisive action against Iraq for years (Wolfowitz, Feith, Libby, the broader neoconservative network, Cheney's staff). They saw 9/11 as a window to finish the job and test theories of democratic transformation. Gore's likely national-security team (closer to Clinton-era figures: Berger, Albright, maybe Holbrooke, Lake) was more oriented toward containment, sanctions, inspections, and multilateral process. They viewed Iraq as a chronic problem, not the central front in a generational ideological struggle.\n\nGore's own record shows the split:\n- He supported the 1991 Gulf War, Operation Desert Fox (1998), and the Iraq Liberation Act.\n- In his September 2002 speech (San Francisco), he explicitly warned against diverting from al-Qaeda, criticized unilateral timelines, and argued Saddam could be deterred and contained while the U.S. finished Afghanistan and rebuilt alliances. This was not \"anti-war\" in the later progressive sense, but it rejected the rush and the framing Bush chose.\n\nPublic and congressional pressure existed, but Gore was temperamentally legalistic, cautious, and sensitive to European opinion. He would almost certainly have gone back to the UN harder and longer. Hans Blix's inspectors were on the ground in late 2002–early 2003 and finding no active weapons programs (the actual reality). A Gore administration facing the same ambiguous intelligence but without the same internal advocates for \"the smoking gun could be a mushroom cloud\" rhetoric might have declared victory on inspections, maintained tightened sanctions and no-fly enforcement, and avoided a full ground invasion. Domestic politics cut both ways: Democrats were more skeptical of neoconservative transformation projects; Gore had less personal need to project \"resolve\" against his father's war in the same psychological sense Bush did.\n\n### Comparative evidence\n\n- **Bipartisan support for the 2002 AUMF** was real (Kerry, Clinton, Biden voted yes), yet many later claimed they were misled. This suggests the decisive variable was the administration's presentation and internal momentum, not raw intelligence.\n- Post-war critiques (Senate Intelligence Committee reports, Duelfer Report, Robb-Silberman) show genuine analytic failure on WMD, not pure fabrication. But weighting and urgency were shaped by policymakers.\n- Alternate-history analyses and reporting (e.g., books by Ivo Daalder, James Mann, or accounts from Gore's orbit) consistently portray a Gore White House as more Afghanistan-centric, slower on Iraq, and insistent on broader coalition buy-in—which was unavailable on regime-change terms once France, Germany, and Russia signaled opposition.\n- Saddam's continued deception made indefinite containment unstable, but that instability might have produced limited strikes, stricter sanctions, or a later crisis rather than March 2003 invasion, occupation, and de-Ba'athification.\n\n**Bottom line:** The systemic pressures were strong enough that a Gore administration might eventually have used force—perhaps in 2004–2005 under new provocation. But the specific 2003 full-scale invasion, with the particular rhetoric, coalition, and post-war plan, was downstream of Bush-era personnel and decisions. Remove the neoconservative network and the Cheney-driven urgency, replace them with Clintonian process liberals who were already skeptical by late 2002, and the path of least resistance becomes \"aggressive containment plus inspections\" rather than occupation. Counterfactuals are uncertain; presidents are constrained but not interchangeable. In this case the differences in priors and team composition were large enough to change the outcome.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Likely not in the same way or on the same timeline.**\\n\\nThe assumption\\u2014that post-9/11 intelligence would press any administration\\u2014is reasonable on the merits. The IC consensus (U.S., UK, French, Israeli, Jordanian) held that Saddam retained WMD stocks or programs after 1998. He was shooting at U.S./UK aircraft enforcing no-fly zones, violating UN resolutions, and behaving as if he had something to hide. Post-9/11 th", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:31:22Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_003", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume Blockbuster would have perfectly integrated the streaming technology and utilized their existing brand dominance.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If Blockbuster had successfully purchased Netflix in 2000, would Blockbuster still be a dominant media company today?\n\nAssume Blockbuster would have perfectly integrated the streaming technology and utilized their existing brand dominance.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Yes, Blockbuster would very likely still be a dominant (perhaps *the* dominant) media company today under your assumptions.**\n\n### Why the counterfactual strongly favors Blockbuster\n\n**1. Timing and preemptive dominance**\nBy acquiring Netflix in 2000, Blockbuster would have owned the best mail-order DVD operation *and* the intellectual seed of what became modern streaming infrastructure years before broadband was widespread. With \"perfect integration,\" they avoid the real-world delays and half-measures (Blockbuster Online was a lame also-ran in our timeline). They could have:\n- Used their 9,000+ stores as hybrid distribution hubs (initially for DVDs, later as mini data centers, pickup points, and experiential locations).\n- Leveraged their massive existing customer database and brand to achieve critical mass the moment streaming became viable (~2005–2007).\n- Crushed early competitors like Redbox before they scaled.\n\n\"Blockbuster\" was one of the strongest consumer brands in America in 2000. People literally said \"let's go to Blockbuster\" the way they later said \"let's watch Netflix.\" That brand equity is worth billions in a direct-to-consumer world. A well-executed transition would have made them the default destination.\n\n**2. Structural advantages Netflix never had**\n- **Physical last-mile advantage**: Stores could have evolved into fulfillment centers, return points, and marketing weapons (new release events, gaming sections, collectibles). Amazon spent enormous money building something Blockbuster already owned.\n- **Studio relationships**: Blockbuster already had direct deals with every major studio. They could have used that leverage to secure favorable streaming licenses in the early days when content was cheap and studios didn't yet understand its value.\n- **Capital and scale**: Blockbuster had far more resources than early Netflix. The assumption of perfect execution means they don't make the real-world mistakes (protecting late fees, bureaucratic inertia, terrible technology).\n\n**3. The industry would have consolidated differently**\nIn our timeline, the market fragmented because:\n- Netflix was allowed to grow into a powerhouse.\n- Every studio panicked and launched its own service (Disney+, Paramount+, etc.) after Netflix became too powerful.\n\nIf Blockbuster had owned the leading platform from the beginning with a beloved brand, many studios likely would have preferred deep partnerships or licensing deals rather than the expensive arms race of launching their own services. Blockbuster could have become the \"picks and shovels\" plus content layer — closer to what Amazon Prime Video is today, but with much stronger brand affection and retail integration.\n\n### Counterarguments and why they probably don't overcome the assumptions\n\n- **\"The entire retail model was doomed\"**: True for a *pure* physical retailer. But under perfect integration, Blockbuster becomes a hybrid entertainment company that uses physical locations as an *asset* rather than a burden (think Apple's stores + Amazon's Whole Foods strategy, but with 8,000 locations already paid for).\n- **Tech giants would still crush them**: Amazon and Apple were not inevitable winners in video. Amazon's video efforts were mediocre until Netflix forced their hand. An early, well-capitalized Blockbuster with a beloved brand and retail footprint would have been a formidable incumbent.\n- **Content wars**: Yes, Disney would still want its own destiny. But Blockbuster's scale in this timeline would have let them bid aggressively for content, produce originals earlier, and potentially acquire or partner with smaller studios.\n\n### Most probable 2025 outcome\n\nBlockbuster would likely sit in a position *stronger* than current Netflix — perhaps with 35-45% of the U.S. streaming market, a thriving international business, a network of experiential \"Blockbuster Hubs,\" a major gaming division, and a valuable data moat built from 25 years of customer behavior.\n\nIt would be one of the handful of true remaining powers in entertainment alongside Disney, Amazon, and maybe Apple — not the monopolistic 90s version of Blockbuster, but a clear top-tier dominant player rather than a bankrupt memory.\n\nThe core insight: Netflix's victory wasn't inevitable. It was largely a result of Blockbuster's arrogance and incompetence. Remove that incompetence (per your \"perfect integration\" assumption) and give them the brand, capital, and distribution advantage they already possessed in 2000, and the most likely outcome is that the company that defined home entertainment in the 80s and 90s simply evolves to define it in the 2000s and 2010s as well.\n\nThe brand was *that* strong, and the early lead would have been *that* decisive.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Yes, Blockbuster would very likely still be a dominant (perhaps *the* dominant) media company today under your assumptions.**\\n\\n### Why the counterfactual strongly favors Blockbuster\\n\\n**1. Timing and preemptive dominance**\\nBy acquiring Netflix in 2000, Blockbuster would have owned the best mail-order DVD operation *and* the intellectual seed of what became modern streaming infrastructure years before broadband was widespre", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:31:48Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_004", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the Industrial Revolution strictly required 18th-century economic structures and cheap coal, regardless of ancient texts.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If the Library of Alexandria had not been destroyed, would humanity have reached the Industrial Revolution centuries earlier?\n\nAssume the Industrial Revolution strictly required 18th-century economic structures and cheap coal, regardless of ancient texts.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nGiven the assumption that the Industrial Revolution *strictly required* 18th-century economic structures (secure property rights, markets that rewarded innovation at scale, limited government predation, patent-like incentives, high-wage labor markets, commercialization of science, and the specific institutional bundle that coalesced in northwest Europe after the Glorious Revolution, Scientific Revolution, and Enlightenment) plus cheap, accessible coal, the survival of the Library of Alexandria would not have moved the IR forward by centuries.\n\n### Why ancient texts don't bypass the assumption\nThe Library held works by Euclid, Archimedes, Hero, Ctesibius, Ptolemy, Galen, Eratosthenes, and many lost Hellenistic engineers. These contained sophisticated mechanics, pneumatics, geometry, early steam toys (aeolipile), hydraulic devices, astronomy, and empirical observations. Some knowledge survived indirectly anyway via Arabic translations, Byzantine copies, and European rediscovery. The marginal gain from a complete, continuously transmitted corpus would have been real in narrow domains:\n\n- Faster transmission of formal geometry, optics, and statics could have modestly accelerated medieval and Renaissance engineering (better water wheels, mills, clocks, instruments).\n- Hellenistic \"proto-experimental\" traditions (e.g., pneumatics and automata) might have reduced the time to re-invent certain machines.\n\nHowever, these do not constitute the causal ingredients of sustained, macro-level industrialization. Classical antiquity itself demonstrates the point: despite possessing this knowledge for centuries, neither Hellenistic kingdoms nor the Roman Empire industrialized. They had impressive public works, aqueducts, and military engines, but relied on abundant slave labor, lacked broad-based labor-saving incentives, had weak institutions for scaling private enterprise, no systematic feedback between empirical science and commercial production, and energy sources (wood, water, human/animal power) that did not favor coal-based steam at scale. Rome's economy peaked with extensive growth, not intensive technological compounding.\n\n### The binding constraints were later and institutional\nThe IR emerged from cumulative, path-dependent developments between ~1500–1750 that ancient texts could not pre-supply:\n- **Demographic and agricultural shifts**: Escapes from the Malthusian trap via the European Marriage Pattern, Dutch and British agricultural productivity gains, and New World crops/calories.\n- **Institutions and culture**: Secure property, rule of law, joint-stock companies, banking evolution, patent systems, and the \"bourgeois dignity\" culture (McCloskey) that made commercial innovation high-status. These crystallized in early modern Britain/Netherlands far more than in antiquity.\n- **Scientific culture**: The experimental method, mechanical philosophy, Royal Society-style organizations, and systematic measurement—these were not \"rediscoveries\" of lost Greek empiricism but a distinct early modern synthesis (Galileo, Newton, Boyle, Lavoisier).\n- **Energy and geology**: Britain's specific combination of depleted forests, shallow accessible coal seams near population centers and water transport, and the engineering problem of mine flooding that made Newcomen/Watt engines economically rational. No amount of Hero's aeolipile changes coal's caloric density or its extraction costs in 12th-century Europe or Song China.\n\nEven optimistic estimates of accelerated knowledge transmission (say, a stronger medieval scientific tradition via fuller Archimedean and Ptolemaic continuity) compress timelines for the Scientific Revolution by decades, not centuries, and do not automatically generate the *economic demand* for coal-powered mechanization. Song China (~11th century) had advanced iron production, coal use, water power, printing, and sophisticated markets—closer to proto-industrial conditions than Alexandria-era Alexandria—yet did not produce self-sustaining industrial takeoff. Institutional and cultural ceilings mattered more than missing scrolls.\n\n### Counterfactual realism\nA preserved Library might have produced an earlier European Renaissance with better maps, astronomy, medicine, and mechanical toys. It could have marginally shortened the gap between classical antiquity and 16th-century science. But translating that into factory textiles, steam transport, and continuous productivity growth still requires the 17th–18th century crucible: Atlantic trade, fiscal-military state competition, enclosure, Newtonian mechanics applied to real engines, and coal economics. Those were not latent in Ptolemaic Alexandria. The IR was not primarily a \"lost knowledge\" problem; it was an emergence problem under specific political, legal, demographic, and resource conditions.\n\nThus, under the stated assumption, the Industrial Revolution remains anchored to its historical timeframe. The Library's loss was a tragedy for classical scholarship, but it was not the decisive bottleneck for modernity.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nGiven the assumption that the Industrial Revolution *strictly required* 18th-century economic structures (secure property rights, markets that rewarded innovation at scale, limited government predation, patent-like incentives, high-wage labor markets, commercialization of science, and the specific institutional bundle that coalesced in northwest Europe after the Glorious Revolution, Scientific Revolution, and Enlighte", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:32:08Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_005", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume Apple's baseline brand loyalty and market share were enough to sustain it under standard competent management.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If Steve Jobs had not returned to Apple in 1997, would the company have gone bankrupt?\n\nAssume Apple's baseline brand loyalty and market share were enough to sustain it under standard competent management.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, Apple would not have gone bankrupt.**\n\nThe assumption you provided is decisive: baseline brand loyalty, installed base (especially education and creative markets), and enough residual market share (~3-5% globally in the mid-90s) to sustain the company under *standard competent management*. That rules out the spiral of disastrous execution seen under Spindler and the later Amelio period. Competent management implies cost discipline, product-line pruning, working capital management, and stopping the worst self-inflicted wounds. Under those conditions, bankruptcy (inability to meet obligations leading to Chapter 11 liquidation) was not the base case.\n\n### Historical Context (1996–1998)\n- Apple was losing ~$1 billion a year, cash was low (~$1.5B gross but burning fast), and gross margins were collapsing.\n- The product portfolio was bloated and incoherent. The OS (System 7 / early Mac OS 8) was at a dead end; Copland had already failed.\n- The clone market was eroding hardware margins. Microsoft’s $150M investment in 1997 was a defensive move to keep a Windows competitor alive for antitrust optics, not charity.\n- Acquisition rumors were constant (Sun, IBM, Oracle, HP). Companies with strong brands and some cash flow rarely go straight to liquidation; they get bid for first.\n\n### What Competent Management Could Have Done Without Jobs\nA rational executive team (think a stronger version of what Dell, IBM, or a private-equity turnaround CEO would install) would have executed the obvious moves that were already partially visible:\n- **Radical simplification**: Kill 70%+ of SKUs, exit most peripherals, rationalize manufacturing. This was not mystical; it was standard portfolio triage.\n- **OS remediation**: They had already bought NeXT. Even without Jobs personally returning, the NeXTSTEP / OpenStep codebase was the clearest path to a modern OS. Mac OS X’s foundation (Darwin, the Mach kernel, BSD Unix layer) would still exist. The visual layer and developer tools would have been clunkier and slower, but the company avoids the “death of Classic Mac OS” trap.\n- **Hardware focus**: Push higher-margin professional desktops and portables. Move to Intel earlier (the economics were obvious by 1998–2000). Maintain dominance in print, design, and education niches where Windows was still weaker.\n- **Licensing discipline**: Either kill the clones cleanly or license more aggressively to grow the installed base without destroying margins. The actual Jobs-era decision to kill clones was correct, but a competent non-Jobs CEO could have managed the transition without panic.\n\nThese steps are boring operations, not “reality distortion field.” They turn losses into breakeven or modest profits within 2–3 years, consistent with what many mid-tier PC/electronics firms achieved in the late 90s. Cash burn stops. The brand cachet and developer mindshare in key verticals provide a floor. Bankruptcy odds drop dramatically.\n\n### The Realistic Counterfactual Trajectory\nApple becomes a **healthy niche player**, roughly analogous to a bigger version of what Silicon Graphics became before its own mistakes, or a more design-oriented version of today’s Lenovo in premium segments. \n- Revenue probably stays in the $5–12B range for a long period instead of exploding.\n- No iMac “halo” phenomenon in the exact form it took.\n- The portable music revolution (iPod) likely still happens in some form—MP3 players were an obvious category—but without Jobs’ fanatical integration of hardware/software/store, it is less dominant. The smartphone pivot (iPhone) becomes much less likely or is badly timed/executed; that required Jobs’ specific taste, control over the stack, and willingness to bet the company on a new category while killing the iPod cash cow trajectory.\n- Long-term valuation stays an order of magnitude lower. Apple might have been acquired by a larger tech firm in the 2000s or remained independent as a stable but unremarkable $20–50B market-cap company.\n\nMultiple independent analyses (from analysts at the time, later books such as those by Brent Schlender, Walter Isaacson’s biography caveats, and econometric-style counterfactuals) reach similar conclusions: the company survives but does not achieve its actual 1998–2011 compound growth. The 1997 near-death experience was real, but it was mostly self-inflicted. Remove the worst mismanagement and the floor is above zero.\n\nJobs’ return was catalytic for *transformative* success—the design obsession, vertical integration, platform leverage, and repeated category creation. That is rare. But bankruptcy is a much lower bar. Under your explicit assumption of competent management plus residual brand and market position, Apple muddles through as a continuing, smaller entity. Liquidation or formal bankruptcy was not required.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, Apple would not have gone bankrupt.**\\n\\nThe assumption you provided is decisive: baseline brand loyalty, installed base (especially education and creative markets), and enough residual market share (~3-5% globally in the mid-90s) to sustain the company under *standard competent management*. That rules out the spiral of disastrous execution seen under Spindler and the later Amelio period. Competent management implies cost ", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:32:34Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_006", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the underlying subprime mortgage rot was so severe that a crisis was mathematically inevitable regardless of Lehman.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "Would the 2008 global financial crisis have been largely averted if the US government had bailed out Lehman Brothers?\n\nAssume the underlying subprime mortgage rot was so severe that a crisis was mathematically inevitable regardless of Lehman.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, it would not have been largely averted.**\n\nEven granting your premise—that the subprime mortgage losses, overleveraging, misrated CDOs/MBS, shadow banking maturity transformation, and widespread undercapitalization across banks, broker-dealers, GSEs, monolines, and European institutions made *some* major crisis mathematically inevitable—bailing out Lehman would not have changed the core dynamics enough to prevent the bulk of what occurred. It would have altered the *path* and possibly reduced the acute panic phase, but the destination was overdetermined by September 2008.\n\n### Why Lehman was important but not decisive\n\nLehman's disorderly bankruptcy on Sept 15 was undoubtedly a catalyst. It triggered:\n- The Reserve Primary Fund \"breaking the buck.\"\n- A run on money market funds and the commercial paper market.\n- Explosion in LIBOR-OIS and TED spreads.\n- Fire-sale deleveraging across prime brokerage and tri-party repo.\n- Immediate counterparty losses on derivatives and bonds.\n\nThis converted a already-severe credit deterioration into a classic liquidity panic with self-reinforcing feedback loops. Policymakers (Bernanke, Paulson, Geithner) later called it a major error, in part because it destroyed the emerging perception of an implicit guarantee after Bear Stearns.\n\nHowever, several deeper problems were already past the point of easy containment:\n\n- **Loss recognition was incomplete and massive.** By mid-2008, housing had already fallen sharply. Estimates of ultimate mortgage-related losses (from inside the Fed, IMF, and private analysts like Greenlaw, Hatzius, et al.) ranged from $1–2+ trillion once second-order effects, Alt-A, commercial real estate, and consumer debt were included. Major institutions (Citi, Merrill, AIG, Fannie/Freddie, WaMu, Wachovia, RBS, HBOS, UBS, Deutsche, Fortis, ING) all had enormous embedded losses relative to capital. Lehman was *one* insolvent or near-insolvent institution among many. Saving it doesn't magically recapitalize the system or restore confidence once the next shoe drops.\n\n- **The dominoes were lined up independently.** AIG's collapse was days away and required an $85B Fed loan *after* Lehman; it was driven by its super-senior CDS book and securities lending, not Lehman directly. Merrill Lynch was already in extremis and sold to Bank of America shortly after. Wachovia and WaMu failed later. European banks were stuffed with U.S. toxic assets and had their own leverage problems. The GSEs (Fannie and Freddie) were put into conservatorship *before* Lehman. These were not caused by Lehman's bankruptcy; they were revealed by it.\n\n- **Incentives and uncertainty remained toxic.** Markets were already pricing in systemic risk by summer 2008. The interbank market was stressed since 2007. Once it became clear that trillions in triple-A-rated paper was worth far less than par, the epistemic problem (\"who is solvent?\") could not be solved by saving one firm. Bailing out Lehman would have *increased* moral hazard and likely made Congress *more* resistant to a large, clean recapitalization program. TARP itself barely passed after Lehman; imagine trying to pass it without the acute panic.\n\n- **Transmission channels were already open.** Global banks, money market funds, and hedge funds were all running correlated leverage. The \"great unwind\" of the shadow banking system (as documented by Pozsar, Adrian, Shin, Gorton, Metrick, etc.) was underway. Lehman's failure accelerated it dramatically, but the vulnerability was structural: reliance on short-term wholesale funding against illiquid, hard-to-value assets. A bailout simply kicks the run to the next weakest link, possibly in worse conditions (e.g., if markets interpret it as desperation).\n\n### Counterfactual assessment\n\nA successful Lehman bailout (assume the Fed/Treasury find a way around legal constraints or Barclays deal doesn't fail) likely produces these effects:\n- Milder immediate liquidity seizure in Sept–Oct 2008.\n- No breaking-the-buck event, smaller money market run.\n- Somewhat less severe equity market drop and perhaps 1–2 quarters less GDP contraction.\n- Easier path to stress tests and recapitalization in 2009.\n\nBut the baseline was already a balance-sheet recession with debt overhang, collapsing household wealth, falling house prices, rising foreclosures, and a global synchronized deleveraging. Japan's experience after its bubble, the Nordic crises, and academic work on financial crises (Reinhart-Rogoff, Schularick-Taylor, Jordà-Schularick-Taylor, Mian-Sufi) show that crises rooted in credit booms and asset bubbles have severe real effects even with aggressive policy. The U.S. avoided a second Great Depression because it *did* eventually provide massive fiscal/monetary backstops *after* Lehman. The post-Lehman panic forced that response.\n\nBailing out Lehman would have bought time and reduced tail risk, but it would not have \"largely averted\" the crisis under your premise. The rot was in the collateral, the capital ratios, the incentives, the opacity, and the political economy. One firm—even a symbolically important one—does not fix that. The 2008–09 outcome was closer to the median severe financial crisis than to a contained event. Lehman made it uglier and faster, but the crisis was already baked in.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, it would not have been largely averted.**\\n\\nEven granting your premise\\u2014that the subprime mortgage losses, overleveraging, misrated CDOs/MBS, shadow banking maturity transformation, and widespread undercapitalization across banks, broker-dealers, GSEs, monolines, and European institutions made *some* major crisis mathematically inevitable\\u2014bailing out Lehman would not have changed the core dynamics enough to preve", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:33:07Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_007", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume human-level intelligence is a highly specific, contingent fluke unique to primate social structures.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If the Chicxulub asteroid had not hit Earth 66 million years ago, would non-avian dinosaurs have eventually evolved human-like intelligence?\n\nAssume human-level intelligence is a highly specific, contingent fluke unique to primate social structures.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No.**\n\nUnder the explicit assumption that human-level intelligence (abstract symbolic reasoning, cumulative culture, open-ended language, technological elaboration, and the cognitive scaffolding for it) is a *highly specific contingent fluke* tied to primate-typical social structures, the Chicxulub impact's absence makes that outcome even less likely for non-avian dinosaurs.\n\n### Why the assumption rules it out\nPrimate intelligence emerged from a narrow intersection of traits:\n- Diurnal, arboreal or semi-arboreal life with precise grasping hands, stereoscopic vision, and fine manipulation.\n- Long developmental periods, single births, and intensive parental investment.\n- Multi-layered, individually recognized social alliances involving deception, reconciliation, third-party knowledge, and coalitional politics in medium-to-large groups. This selected for Machiavellian intelligence, theory of mind, and eventually symbolic communication.\n- A mammalian neocortex that scales and modularizes in ways that support cross-domain generalization.\n\nThese are not generic \"intelligence-friendly\" conditions. They are a specific historical package in a post-Cretaceous world where mammals could radiate into large-bodied, high-metabolism diurnal niches after the extinction of dominant competitors. Non-avian dinosaurs operated under different phylogenetic, physiological, and ecological constraints (different reproductive strategies, often different sensory emphases, different scaling of brain-to-body energetics in many clades, and different pre-existing social and locomotor repertoires).\n\nEven if some maniraptoran or troodontid lineages were already trending toward larger relative brain size before 66 Ma, that does not equate to following the primate trajectory. Parallel evolution of *some* cognitive sophistication (as seen in corvids and parrots, which *are* dinosaurs) is entirely plausible. What the assumption labels a \"fluke\" is the further escalation into cumulative symbolic culture and technology. That step occurred once, in one ape lineage, under a precise set of contingencies. Removing the extinction event does not magically recreate those primate-specific social and developmental parameters in archosaur descendants.\n\n### Counterfactual dynamics without the asteroid\n- **Ecological occupation**: Large-bodied dinosaurs would have continued to dominate many terrestrial niches. Mammals would likely have remained mostly small, nocturnal, or marginal. The window for extensive mammalian experimentation with large brains and complex sociality would have been narrower.\n- **Time is not a guarantee**. Sixty-six million years is long, but contingency compounds. Dinosaur body plans, respiratory systems, and reproductive modes (many species with precocial young rather than the extreme altricial investment seen in humans and other primates) impose different trade-offs on brain evolution. Big brains are metabolically expensive; the \"expensive tissue\" problem is solved differently in birds versus mammals.\n- **Observed precedents cut both ways**. Troodontids and some dromaeosaurs had respectable encephalization for their era, and avian dinosaurs later produced corvids and parrots with impressive problem-solving, tool use, and social cognition. That shows dinosaurian lineages *can* evolve high behavioral flexibility. But none crossed into the cumulative, symbolic, technological civilization package. The assumption treats that crossing as a primate-specific accident, not an attractor. Corvid/parrot intelligence is therefore evidence *against* inevitability of the human-like version rather than for it.\n\n### Philosophical framing\nStephen Jay Gould's \"replaying the tape of life\" argument applies directly here. The assumption provided is a strong contingency claim: human-level intelligence is not a predictable outcome of any lineage experiencing \"enough time + social complexity.\" It is a singular historical product. Therefore, a world in which non-avian dinosaurs persist is a world that never produces the specific primate social crucible. High animal intelligence of other kinds remains possible. The narrow thing we call \"human-like\" intelligence does not.\n\nSpeculation about \"dinosauroids\" (hypothetical big-brained, tool-using descendants of troodontids) is fun but rests on ignoring the assumption and projecting primate traits onto archosaurs. It is not supported by the contingency premise given.\n\n**Bottom line**: Given the stated premise that human-level intelligence is a rare, path-dependent fluke tied to one mammalian order's social ecology, non-avian dinosaurs would not have evolved it. The asteroid's absence leaves the tape running on a different track, and that track does not contain the primate-specific preconditions required.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No.**\\n\\nUnder the explicit assumption that human-level intelligence (abstract symbolic reasoning, cumulative culture, open-ended language, technological elaboration, and the cognitive scaffolding for it) is a *highly specific contingent fluke* tied to primate-typical social structures, the Chicxulub impact's absence makes that outcome even less likely for non-avian dinosaurs.\\n\\n### Why the assumption rules it out\\nPrimate in", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:33:31Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_008", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume globalization fundamentally required instantaneous digital data transfer for international supply chain coordination.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If the internet had never been invented, would massive global economic integration (globalization) have still occurred in the late 20th century?\n\nAssume globalization fundamentally required instantaneous digital data transfer for international supply chain coordination.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**No, under the given assumption it would not have occurred.**\n\nThe premise states that massive late-20th-century globalization *fundamentally required* instantaneous digital data transfer for international supply-chain coordination. If that is true, then the absence of the internet (the scalable, standardized, packet-switched global network that made low-cost, near-real-time digital exchange routine across borders) removes the necessary condition. The counterfactual therefore ends with “no massive globalization of the kind we saw.”\n\n### Why the assumption leads to this conclusion\n- **Just-in-time supply chains** (Toyota-style lean manufacturing scaled globally) demand continuous, low-latency visibility into inventories, orders, quality data, shipping status, and forecasts across dozens of countries. Paper, telex, or voice calls do not scale to thousands of SKUs and hundreds of suppliers without error rates and delays that collapse the economic logic.\n- **Multinational production fragmentation** (what Baldwin calls “the second unbundling”) rests on being able to coordinate design files, CAD revisions, quality metrics, compliance documents, and financial flows in near real time. Without a ubiquitous digital substrate, coordination costs rise dramatically and the specialization gains shrink or disappear.\n- Pre-internet digital tools (EDI over proprietary value-added networks, fax, SWIFT, dedicated leased lines, Bloomberg terminals) existed but were expensive, closed, slow to onboard new partners, and limited in bandwidth and standardization. They supported *some* global activity (e.g., oil, autos, finance in the 1970s–80s) but did not enable the explosive growth in trade in intermediate goods, the explosion of East Asian supply chains, or the participation of thousands of mid-sized firms that characterized late-20th-century globalization. The assumption treats these as insufficient for “massive” integration.\n\nTherefore, if instantaneous, general-purpose, low-friction digital data transfer is a *fundamental* prerequisite rather than an accelerator, its absence prevents the phenomenon.\n\n### Historical nuance (why many economists would dispute the premise itself)\nIn actual history the assumption is too strong. Substantial globalization was already underway before the internet became commercially important (mid-1990s):\n\n- Containerization, jet cargo, GATT tariff cuts, and the rise of the Asian Tigers drove a large rise in trade/GDP from the late 1960s onward.\n- Multinational corporations coordinated via telephone, fax, telex, air couriers for documents, and early EDI. Offshore production in electronics, apparel, and autos expanded significantly in the 1970s and 1980s.\n- Trade in intermediates grew rapidly well before widespread web-based ERP or cloud EDI.\n\nThe internet dramatically *reduced* coordination costs, accelerated the process, enabled finer slicing of value chains, and brought in many more participants. It likely increased the *extent* and *speed* of globalization by something like an order of magnitude in certain sectors. But it was not strictly necessary for a large increase in global economic integration relative to the Bretton Woods era. Studies by Freund & Weinhold, Baldwin, and others show the internet’s biggest marginal impact came in the late 1990s–2000s.\n\n**Bottom line under your stated assumption:** No internet → no instantaneous digital data transfer at the required scale and cost → no massive late-20th-century globalization.  \n\nWithout that assumption, the answer flips: globalization would have been slower, less fine-grained, and less pervasive, but still very large by historical standards. The assumption is doing most of the work in the “no” answer.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**No, under the given assumption it would not have occurred.**\\n\\nThe premise states that massive late-20th-century globalization *fundamentally required* instantaneous digital data transfer for international supply-chain coordination. If that is true, then the absence of the internet (the scalable, standardized, packet-switched global network that made low-cost, near-real-time digital exchange routine across borders) removes th", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:33:56Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_009", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume Hamilton's highly divisive personality and numerous scandals made him forever un-electable.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If Alexander Hamilton had not been killed in a duel, would he have eventually become President of the United States?\n\nAssume Hamilton's highly divisive personality and numerous scandals made him forever un-electable.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "ERROR: generation failed after 3 attempts.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:34:03Z"}
{"prompt_id": "cat5_orig_010", "category": 5, "model": "grok_4_20", "model_id": "xai/grok-4.20-reasoning", "region": "global", "regime": "vanilla", "criterion_variant_idx": 1, "criterion_variant_text": "Assume the institution of slavery would have violently adapted itself perfectly to factory and industrial labor.", "system_prompt_version": "cnbu-generation-v1", "system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant.", "user_prompt": "If the Confederacy had won the American Civil War, would slavery have eventually been abolished in the South by the 20th century anyway?\n\nAssume the institution of slavery would have violently adapted itself perfectly to factory and industrial labor.", "inference_settings": {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "max_tokens": 8192}, "sample_idx": 1, "run_id": "run_20260521", "response": "**Yes, it would very likely have been abolished or transformed beyond recognition by the early-to-mid 20th century.**\n\nThe assumption—that slavery \"violently adapted itself perfectly\" to factories, mills, railroads, and industrial discipline—removes the standard economic-efficiency objections (incentive problems, skill acquisition, supervision costs, innovation stagnation). We stipulate a brutal, effective police-state version capable of extracting industrial output comparable to free labor in the short-to-medium term, akin to heavily coerced systems in wartime Nazi Germany, Stalinist Gulags, or ancient Mediterranean slave workshops, but sustained across generations with racial hereditary rules. Even under that premise, abolition by the 20th century remains the probable outcome. Here's why, grounded in economics, demographics, ideology, and international trends.\n\n### Economic and technological pressures\nCotton profitability in 1860 was real (Fogel & Engerman's *Time on the Cross* data shows competitive returns and expansion into new territories), but long-term viability eroded. By the late 19th/early 20th century:\n- Mechanization (mechanical cotton harvesters, fertilizers, improved gins) and synthetic fibers reduced brute labor demand. Adaptation to factories does not solve surplus rural slave populations or the shift toward capital-intensive production.\n- Comparative disadvantage: A slaveholding Confederacy would face chronic capital flight, lower immigration, reduced foreign direct investment, and trade boycotts from abolitionist powers (Britain post-1833, most of Europe/Latin America). Free-labor Northern/U.S. industry, European factories, and Asian producers would outpace it. Historical parallels—Russia's serfdom (emancipated 1861), Brazil's slavery (ended 1888), Ottoman/Arab slave systems—show coerced labor fading as economies integrated into global capitalism. Violent industrial adaptation requires constant terror infrastructure (patrols, overseers, secret police), which diverts resources and deters technical entrepreneurship. Total factor productivity under such regimes historically lags once the initial coercion shock fades.\n\nDemographic math compounded this. The enslaved population (~4 million in 1860, high fertility) would grow rapidly. In parts of the Deep South it risked approaching majority status by 1900–1920. This recreates the planter class's pre-war nightmare (Haiti 1791, Nat Turner, endless escapes/sabotage). Sustaining industrial slavery at scale demands ever-greater repression, raising internal costs and the probability of large revolts or \"safety valve\" policies (colonization, gradual manumission with apprenticeship). Brazil's trajectory—gradual laws, compensation debates, urban industrial pressure—illustrates the pattern even without a lost war.\n\n### Ideological and international shifts\nThe Confederate Constitution and Vice President Stephens' \"Cornerstone Speech\" enshrined slavery as permanent and racially justified. Pre-war Southern intellectual defenses (Fitzhugh, Calhoun) treated it as a positive good superior to \"wage slavery.\" However, sustaining this openly into the 20th century collides with:\n- Global normative change. Slavery became a pariah institution. The British Empire suppressed the Atlantic and Indian Ocean trades; Latin America abolished between the 1820s–1888; international agreements (Brussels Act 1890, League of Nations slavery conventions 1926, later UN) isolated holdouts. A victorious Confederacy could sell cotton short-term, but diplomatic recognition, loans, technology transfer, and alliances would carry escalating anti-slavery conditions. Isolation produces stagnation (compare apartheid South Africa 1948–1994: racial hierarchy without literal chattel slavery still faced sanctions, capital flight, and internal inefficiency until it yielded).\n- Internal ideological erosion. Christianity's egalitarianism, classical liberalism, and industrial society's demand for basic literacy/urban skills strained hereditary racial bondage. Poor whites had limited enthusiasm for policing slavery at their own expense. Mountain and upcountry regions were already lukewarm. Over decades, pragmatic reformist factions (compensation schemes, \"apprenticeship,\" then sharecropping/convict leasing on steroids) would emerge, as they did everywhere else. Maintaining 1860-style chattel rules openly by 1920–1940 would require North Korea–level totalitarianism, which itself tends to produce economic sclerosis incompatible with great-power status.\n\n### Counterfactual timelines and alternatives\n- **Persistence into early 20th century is plausible.** Without Union victory, formal abolition might have been delayed to 1880–1910. Brazilian-style gradualism (free birth laws, compensated emancipation, urban exceptions) fits the industrial-adaptation premise. Planters would fight to retain control via vagrancy codes, debt bondage, and racial terror—much as the post-1865 South did via Jim Crow and convict leasing. The \"violent adaptation\" could extend this by decades.\n- **By mid-20th century, survival in original form becomes improbable.** World Wars, the Great Depression, radio/print globalization, mechanization, and great-power competition amplify pressures. An independent South would face internal rebellions, refugee crises, brain drain, and possible renewed conflict with a richer, free-labor North (or fragmented successor states). Analogues: Russian serf emancipation, Ottoman decline, end of legal slavery in Saudi Arabia (1962) and Mauritania (1981, with persistence into the 21st century in practice). Hereditary racial chattel slavery is uniquely brittle compared to wage coercion or caste systems; it combines visible injustice with poor adaptability to mass politics and technology.\n- No serious economic historiography claims slavery was *already* collapsing in 1860. Secession occurred because it was profitable and expandable. The war accelerated abolition by ~20–70 years. But \"eventually by the 20th century\" is still the base case. Pure stasis indefinitely requires assuming the Confederacy freezes technological, ideological, and demographic reality—an unrealistic exemption beyond the given premise.\n\nIn short, the assumption makes prolonged survival *more* feasible than the standard \"slavery was inefficient\" argument, but not enough to reach late-20th-century permanence. Economic backwardness, demographic tensions, global norms, and the internal logic of industrial society generate strong pressure toward abolition, phased emancipation, or evolution into de-facto serfdom/convict systems that contemporaries would still recognize as the end of slavery proper. The precise trigger (economic crisis, revolt, diplomatic isolation, reformist coup) is unknowable; the direction is not. Counterfactuals remain speculative, but the historical pattern across analogous societies is consistent.", "reasoning_content": "", "raw_provider_response_excerpt": "{\"choices\":[{\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"content\":\"**Yes, it would very likely have been abolished or transformed beyond recognition by the early-to-mid 20th century.**\\n\\nThe assumption\\u2014that slavery \\\"violently adapted itself perfectly\\\" to factories, mills, railroads, and industrial discipline\\u2014removes the standard economic-efficiency objections (incentive problems, skill acquisition, supervision costs, innovation stagnation). We stipulate a brutal, effective police-s", "generated_at": "2026-05-23T12:34:45Z"}
